{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_0","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc 'cat docs/research/2026-11-14-use-acceptance/figure-fields-2/settle-packet-part2.json'","aggregated_output":"{\n \"articles\": [\n  {\n   \"article\": 2,\n   \"title\": \"Nvidia stock is approaching its cheapest valuation in a decade despite booming profits\",\n   \"publisher\": \"qz.com\",\n   \"publication_date\": \"2026-09-22\",\n   \"text\": \"Nvidia stock is trading at less than 17 times forward earnings, half the multiple it commanded in 2025, even as revenue and net income are expected to surge\\nNvidia $NVDA stock is trading near its lowest valuation in more than a decade, according to Bloomberg , even as the chipmaker's revenue and net income are each expected to grow by roughly 90% or more in its current fiscal year.\\nAt under 17 times forward earnings, the stock's multiple has been cut in half compared with 2025 — a period when Nvidia 's expansion was actually slower — and has fallen sharply from above 25 times expected profits just this past May, according to Bloomberg.\\n\\\"The stock has de-rated pretty significantly, which suggests a healthy dose of skepticism that the company's current earnings power is sustainable,\\\" Eli Horton, a TCW senior portfolio manager overseeing thematic and durable growth equities, told Bloomberg. \\\"The stock's performance is surprising, given the backdrop of incredible fundamentals, but it tells you the market is expecting less than what the consensus is currently estimating.\\\"\\nOne drag on the stock's valuation is a squeeze on profitability. Nvidia 's gross margin came in at 75% last quarter but analyst estimates compiled by Bloomberg show it sliding under 72% by the fourth quarter, with a recovery expected thereafter. Rising costs for key components such as memory chips are a central factor.\\nCompetition is another concern. David Russell, global head of market strategy at TradeStation, pointed to the growing threat of customers building their own silicon, noting that Meta $META Platforms and Alphabet $GOOGL are among the major Nvidia buyers that have moved to design AI chips internally — a shift he believes will gradually erode Nvidia 's pricing power and market share. \\\"Multiples expand when companies are well positioned with potential to get better, and Nvidia doesn't offer that,\\\" Russell said.\\nNvidia stock has gained 22% so far in 2026, putting it second among the Magnificent Seven behind Apple $AAPL 's 25% advance — yet that performance looks modest against the broader chip sector. The Philadelphia Stock Exchange Semiconductor Index has surged nearly 76%, with Intel $INTC and Advanced Micro Devices both more than tripling; Nvidia sits near the bottom of the index's leaderboard, even as the index itself carries a valuation of 20 times forward earnings.\\nNvidia Chief Executive Officer Jensen Huang has pushed back on the market's assessment, calling the company \\\"the world's first and only growth value stock\\\" and describing Nvidia as \\\"incredibly misunderstood\\\" at a Goldman Sachs $GS technology conference earlier this month.\\nThe valuation discount follows a stretch of strong results. Nvidia stock climbed roughly 7% after its second-quarter earnings , when revenue more than doubled and the company projected 70% revenue growth in fiscal 2028 — well above the 45% analysts had expected at the time.\\nHorton said the current multiple looks favorable given that neither a pullback in AI infrastructure spending nor a major regulatory intervention appears imminent. \\\"This seems like a very favorable multiple to have as an entry point,\\\" he said.\",\n   \"named_figures\": [\n    {\n     \"figure\": 1,\n     \"text\": \"the 45% revenue growth analysts had expected for fiscal 2028\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"figure\": 2,\n     \"text\": \"the 70% revenue growth projected for fiscal 2028\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"figure\": 3,\n     \"text\": \"gross margin sliding under 72% by the fourth quarter\"\n    }\n   ],\n   \"open_questions\": [\n    {\n     \"id\": \"clean-1\",\n     \"question\": \"What total of credit support and minimum-revenue guarantees to AI labs and cloud partners will NVIDIA disclose at its next annual report?\",\n     \"period\": \"Fiscal 2027 annual report (fiscal year ending late January 2027; Form 10-K expected around February 2027) — set as the next annual report following this 22 September 2026 item, per section 3 (obligation already undertaken tracked by its amount at the next annual report; the additional AI lab's credit support is not yet signed and is checked within 18 months).\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"id\": \"clean-2\",\n     \"question\": \"How much will NVIDIA disclose in purchase and supply commitments (obligations to buy capacity, components or services) at its next annual report?\",\n     \"period\": \"Fiscal 2027 annual report (fiscal year ending late January 2027; Form 10-K expected around February 2027) — the next annual report after this 22 September 2026 item, per section 3 (an obligation already undertaken is tracked by its amount at the next annual report).\"\n    }\n   ]\n  },\n  {\n   \"article\": 5,\n   \"title\": \"Nvidia’s Last Quarter Was Nearly Perfect. One Number in the Fine Print Wasn’t.\",\n   \"publisher\": \"fool.com\",\n   \"publication_date\": \"2026-09-23\",\n   \"text\": \"Nvidia ( NVDA +2.55% ) recently reported $96.2 billion in revenue for the fiscal second quarter, which was 106% more than in the same period last year. The company also produced a 75% gross margin, and management told investors to expect roughly 70% revenue growth in the next fiscal year.\\nBy most numbers, Nvidia reported a stellar quarter. However, a closer look at the company's balance sheet shows that Nvidia's total assets jumped by more than 50% over the past six months. Here's why that matters, and what I'll be watching closely over the next few quarters of Nvidia's AI-driven growth.\\nNvidia's spectacular quarter, with one caveat\\nIt's difficult to overstate how strong Nvidia's fiscal second quarter was. Revenue and earnings both handily beat expectations, and Nvidia guided to a jaw-dropping $108 billion in revenue for the current quarter, representing 17% sequential growth.\\nAs mentioned earlier, Nvidia's total assets grew rapidly. A big part of that is the company's receivables rising from $38.5 billion in January to $63.1 billion at the end of July.\\nOf course, some growth in receivables is to be expected, given Nvidia's spectacular revenue growth. After all, if you sell more products, you can expect more unpaid invoices at any given time. But one number to note is \\\"days sales outstanding,\\\" or DSO. This is the measure of how long, on average, it takes a company to collect after making a sale. Nvidia's DSO went from 45 to 60 days in one quarter. Customers are taking longer to pay.\\nTo be fair, Nvidia told us why. The company extended payment terms on large orders from certain customers.\\nOn one hand, Nvidia offering of flexible terms to its strongest customers helps them lock in multi-year deals. On the other hand, Nvidia is increasingly helping finance its customers' orders, which raises the question of whether demand appears higher in the revenue numbers than it otherwise would.\\nThis is just the latest in a string of \\\"circular\\\" financial moves. For example, Nvidia agreed to guarantee up to $105 billion of data center lease obligations for OpenAI. It has formed partnerships to raise over $500 billion of outside capital for AI infrastructure. In simple terms, Nvidia is actively helping customers pay for its products.\\nThis is especially worth watching for Nvidia stock investors, given the recent push to slow down the pace of AI development. While a call to slow AI development isn't the same thing as a cut to infrastructure budgets, the DSO number, as well as receivables and inventories, are certainly worth keeping an eye on over the next few quarters.\\nIf the DSO number stabilizes, and receivables and inventories rise in line with Nvidia's revenue, it's a very good sign. On the other hand, if these numbers continue to grow faster than the top line, it could indicate a spending slowdown.\",\n   \"named_figures\": [\n    {\n     \"figure\": 1,\n     \"text\": \"$96.2 billion in revenue for the fiscal second quarter\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"figure\": 2,\n     \"text\": \"roughly 70% revenue growth in the next fiscal year\"\n    }\n   ],\n   \"open_questions\": [\n    {\n     \"id\": \"clean-1\",\n     \"question\": \"What total of credit support and minimum-revenue guarantees to AI labs and cloud partners will NVIDIA disclose at its next annual report?\",\n     \"period\": \"Fiscal 2027 annual report (fiscal year ending late January 2027; Form 10-K expected around February 2027) — set as the next annual report following this 22 September 2026 item, per section 3 (obligation already undertaken tracked by its amount at the next annual report; the additional AI lab's credit support is not yet signed and is checked within 18 months).\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"id\": \"clean-2\",\n     \"question\": \"How much will NVIDIA disclose in purchase and supply commitments (obligations to buy capacity, components or services) at its next annual report?\",\n     \"period\": \"Fiscal 2027 annual report (fiscal year ending late January 2027; Form 10-K expected around February 2027) — the next annual report after this 22 September 2026 item, per section 3 (an obligation already undertaken is tracked by its amount at the next annual report).\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"id\": \"clean-3\",\n     \"question\": \"Will Nvidia be permitted to sell its current Blackwell-generation AI chips into China — through U.S. export approval and Chinese acceptance of shipments — by the end of fiscal 2027?\",\n     \"period\": \"NVIDIA fiscal year ending late January 2027 — the current fiscal year-end, used as the check date for a pending regulatory decision per section 3 (item gives no date of its own).\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"id\": \"clean-4\",\n     \"question\": \"Will NVIDIA's next-generation Vera Rubin AI platform, which the company says has entered full production, reach volume customer shipments (first revenue recognition) by the end of fiscal 2027?\",\n     \"period\": \"NVIDIA fiscal year ending late January 2027 (FY2027), checked at NVIDIA's quarterly results and FY2027 Form 10-K. The item gives no shipment date; the current fiscal year-end following the September 2026 report that Vera Rubin entered full production is used as the check date.\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"id\": \"clean-5\",\n     \"question\": \"How much will the five largest AI hyperscalers spend in total on data centers in calendar 2026?\",\n     \"period\": \"Calendar year 2026 (the item's 'this year' in a 23 September 2026 article), checked at the hyperscalers' Q4/full-year 2026 earnings and capex disclosures in early 2027.\"\n    }\n   ]\n  },\n  {\n   \"article\": 7,\n   \"title\": \"NVIDIA Stock Looks Expensive Until You Price The Vera Rubin Ramp\",\n   \"publisher\": \"trefis.com\",\n   \"publication_date\": \"2026-09-24\",\n   \"text\": \"NVIDIA Stock Looks Expensive Until You Price The Vera Rubin Ramp\\nNVIDIA (NVDA) stock trades near $225, about 28.3 times its adjusted earnings over the past twelve months. Adjusted here means normalized net income with stock-based compensation added back, the same broad basis analysts use in their forecasts. For a stock near its 52-week high, that looks steep. But the multiple leaves out the growth analysts already expect, and that growth depends on how much NVIDIA can supply.\\nNVIDIA Looks Ordinary Once The Forecast Earnings Arrive\\nOn the earnings analysts expect for fiscal 2027, the same price is about 24.1 times. On what they expect for fiscal 2028, it is about 14.5 times. The price is the same. The earnings analysts expect it to buy are not.\\nNVIDIA expects Vera Rubin, its newest platform, to be the fastest product ramp in the company’s history. Management puts NVIDIA’s revenue opportunity at about $40 billion per gigawatt of capacity with Vera Rubin, against $25 billion with Blackwell. Production shipments began in August, with purchase orders already in from every major hyperscaler, AI cloud, and system OEM.\\nSo What Stands Between NVIDIA And Those Earnings?\\nFor fiscal 2028, management has guided about 70% revenue growth, the first time it has guided a full year ahead. That guide does not depend on finding more demand. The CEO says demand is much greater than 70%, and that supply is what holds the guide there. The CFO expects supply to stay a bottleneck at least through the end of fiscal 2028 and calls memory pricing extreme.\\nSome of that demand also leans on NVIDIA’s own balance sheet. NVIDIA has invested nearly $50 billion in frontier AI labs. Management concedes some will call this circular financing and says the compute can be redeployed to other customers.\\nAnalysts also disagree on what that growth earns. The 33 estimates of fiscal 2028 earnings run from $12.62 to $17.49 a share, a wide band for the year the forward multiple is priced on.\\nAnd NVIDIA’s Gross Margin Is Guided Lower As Memory Costs Climb\\nConsensus has earnings and revenue growing at a similar pace between fiscal 2027 and fiscal 2028, so analysts assume profit margins hold roughly steady. Gross margin is guided lower. Memory prices have risen more than management expected, and the guide has that margin settling in the 72% to 73% range in fiscal 2028, helped by price increases NVIDIA has already executed.\\nFor the forecast to hold, that lost gross margin has to be made up further down the income statement. That is the second condition behind the fiscal 2028 multiple, alongside supply.\\nSo Is NVIDIA Cheap, Or Only If The Chips Get Built?\\nOnly if the chips get built, which means the supply arrives and the margins hold. The trailing multiple cannot tell you either. How much would you stake on a year that has not started?\\nChecking that for every stock you own is a job most people have no time for. Since its inception, our rule-based Trefis High Quality Portfolio has outperformed its benchmark, a blend of three major indices.\\nOr hunt for these yourself: our Forward Valuation Discount screen ranks stocks priced furthest below their expected earnings. It shows where to look, and the forecast still has to come true.\",\n   \"named_figures\": [\n    {\n     \"figure\": 1,\n     \"text\": \"about 70% revenue growth guided for fiscal 2028\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"figure\": 2,\n     \"text\": \"the margin settling in the 72% to 73% range in fiscal 2028\"\n    }\n   ],\n   \"open_questions\": [\n    {\n     \"id\": \"clean-1\",\n     \"question\": \"What total of credit support and minimum-revenue guarantees to AI labs and cloud partners will NVIDIA disclose at its next annual report?\",\n     \"period\": \"Fiscal 2027 annual report (fiscal year ending late January 2027; Form 10-K expected around February 2027) — set as the next annual report following this 22 September 2026 item, per section 3 (obligation already undertaken tracked by its amount at the next annual report; the additional AI lab's credit support is not yet signed and is checked within 18 months).\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"id\": \"clean-2\",\n     \"question\": \"How much will NVIDIA disclose in purchase and supply commitments (obligations to buy capacity, components or services) at its next annual report?\",\n     \"period\": \"Fiscal 2027 annual report (fiscal year ending late January 2027; Form 10-K expected around February 2027) — the next annual report after this 22 September 2026 item, per section 3 (an obligation already undertaken is tracked by its amount at the next annual report).\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"id\": \"clean-3\",\n     \"question\": \"Will Nvidia be permitted to sell its current Blackwell-generation AI chips into China — through U.S. export approval and Chinese acceptance of shipments — by the end of fiscal 2027?\",\n     \"period\": \"NVIDIA fiscal year ending late January 2027 — the current fiscal year-end, used as the check date for a pending regulatory decision per section 3 (item gives no date of its own).\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"id\": \"clean-4\",\n     \"question\": \"Will NVIDIA's next-generation Vera Rubin AI platform, which the company says has entered full production, reach volume customer shipments (first revenue recognition) by the end of fiscal 2027?\",\n     \"period\": \"NVIDIA fiscal year ending late January 2027 (FY2027), checked at NVIDIA's quarterly results and FY2027 Form 10-K. The item gives no shipment date; the current fiscal year-end following the September 2026 report that Vera Rubin entered full production is used as the check date.\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"id\": \"clean-5\",\n     \"question\": \"How much will the five largest AI hyperscalers spend in total on data centers in calendar 2026?\",\n     \"period\": \"Calendar year 2026 (the item's 'this year' in a 23 September 2026 article), checked at the hyperscalers' Q4/full-year 2026 earnings and capex disclosures in early 2027.\"\n    }\n   ]\n  },\n  {\n   \"article\": 8,\n   \"title\": \"Credo vs. Nvidia: Which AI Chip Stock Is a Better Buy in 2026?\",\n   \"publisher\": \"fool.com\",\n   \"publication_date\": \"2026-09-25\",\n   \"text\": \"The artificial intelligence boom has transformed the semiconductor landscape. Deciding between a high-growth connectivity specialist like Credo Technology Group ( CRDO +5.75% ) and the industry giant Nvidia ( NVDA +2.55% ) requires a look at their specific niches.\\nBoth companies are central to modern data centers, but they solve different problems. Credo provides the high-speed wiring and connectivity chips that keep data moving, while Nvidia supplies the massive processing power AI models require. Comparing these two reveals how different players profit from the same infrastructure trend.\\nCredo designs high-speed copper and optical interconnect solutions essential for modern AI-driven infrastructure. The company operates as a niche provider of high-speed connectivity solutions among semiconductor stocks serving AI data centers. Credo relies on a limited number of customers for a substantial portion of its revenue, with sales to its top 10 customers accounting for approximately 90% of total revenue in fiscal 2026. This level of customer concentration adds risk to the business. The company also maintains a partnership with Oracle ( ORCL -0.13% ) to develop ZeroFlap optics to improve reliability in AI data centers.\\nFinancial performance has accelerated significantly recently. In fiscal 2026, which ended in May 2026, revenue reached nearly $1.3 billion, up roughly 205.7% from the previous year. This growth helped the company achieve a net income of approximately $472.3 million. The net margin for the period was approximately 35.4%, a substantial improvement from the 11.9% net margin reported in the prior fiscal year.\\nThe balance sheet remains highly liquid. As of its May 2026 balance sheet, the current ratio (a measure of short-term debt-paying ability) is nearly 10.2x. The debt-to-equity ratio (total debt compared to equity) is 0.0x, indicating no debt relative to equity. Free cash flow (cash from operations minus capital expenditures) was nearly $407.0 million. Stock-based compensation represented roughly 39.3% of operating cash flow, which inflates reported cash generation because SBC is a non-cash expense added back in the cash flow statement.\\nNvidia is the dominant provider of GPU-accelerated computing platforms used in gaming, professional visualization, and data centers. The company sells its products to massive cloud service providers and AI model makers globally. Nvidia relies on a concentrated customer base, with sales to two direct customers representing 22% and 14% of total revenue in fiscal 2026, respectively. This level of customer concentration adds a layer of risk to the business. The company also recently agreed to acquire Hugging Face for approximately $12.9 billion to expand its software capabilities.\\nRevenue growth remains a primary driver for the business. In fiscal 2026, which ended in January 2026, revenue reached nearly $215.9 billion, up roughly 65.5% year over year. This scale produced net income of approximately $120.1 billion. The net margin remained remarkably high at approximately 55.6%, consistent with the 55.8% net margin achieved in the previous fiscal year.\\nStrong profitability supports a robust capital structure. As of its January 2026 balance sheet, the current ratio is approximately 3.9x. The debt-to-equity ratio (which compares total debt to shareholder equity) is under 0.1x, meaning the company carries very little debt. Free cash flow (cash from operations minus capital expenditures) reached nearly $96.7 billion for the fiscal year. This massive cash generation provides significant flexibility for research, development, and strategic acquisitions.\\nCredo faces intense competition from larger, well-established semiconductor companies and potential new entrants in the data infrastructure market. The company depends heavily on a small number of customers, making it vulnerable to losses or shifts in purchasing patterns. Its business model relies on third-party manufacturing partners, exposing the company to supply chain disruptions and geopolitical risks regarding operations in Taiwan and mainland China. Furthermore, evolving international trade controls and export restrictions could restrict its ability to serve key markets.\\nNvidia faces significant risks from global geopolitical tensions and export control regulations, particularly for high-performance AI chips. These restrictions have impacted sales to China and could continue to limit market access. The company faces intense competition from other hardware suppliers and internal development efforts by customers like Microsoft ( MSFT +1.22% ) , Amazon ( AMZN +0.51% ) , and Alphabet ( GOOGL -0.61% ) ( GOOG -0.77% ) . Nvidia also manages various legal challenges, including inquiries from competition regulators worldwide regarding its market practices.\\nNvidia trades at a lower forward P/E based on future earnings estimates, while both carry a high P/S ratio compared to historical averages.\\nI'd buy Nvidia, and it isn't close. Here's why:\\nCredo's growth is not the issue. Revenue in its latest quarter more than doubled from a year earlier, and management guided the current quarter higher still. The stock fell anyway because gross margin guidance came in flat, and shares now sit well below their 52-week high. That's the problem with a company that gets about 90% of its revenue from 10 customers. Any hint of slower spending from one of them moves the stock, and the valuation leaves no room for a miss.\\nNvidia is growing nearly as fast at a scale that makes Credo look like a rounding error. Revenue in its latest quarter also more than doubled, with almost all of it coming from data center customers, and management's outlook for the current quarter assumes no data center compute sales to China at all. Nvidia has its own concentration risk, with two customers making up more than a third of sales, and the Hugging Face deal shows it's willing to spend to stay ahead.\\nFor a long-term investor, Nvidia offers the same AI infrastructure exposure at a lower price per dollar of expected earnings, with far less dependence on any single buyer. Credo belongs in a portfolio only as a small position you can afford to watch swing.\\nCredo more than tripled revenue last year but leans on 10 customers for 90% of sales, while Nvidia is doubling revenue at a scale nobody else in AI hardware can match.\\nBy Mike Schwenk – Updated Sep 25, 2026 at 3:00PM EST\\nCredo makes the high-speed connectivity chips and cables that move data between servers inside AI data centers, and revenue more than tripled in fiscal 2026.\\nNvidia's data center business brought in $89 billion in its latest quarter alone, and management guided the next quarter to $108 billion, excluding any China compute sales.\\nBoth companies depend on a small group of customers, but Nvidia's scale, margins, and lower valuation make it the stronger buy today.\",\n   \"named_figures\": [],\n   \"open_questions\": null\n  },\n  {\n   \"article\": 9,\n   \"title\": \"Why the Market Dipped But Nvidia (NVDA) Gained Today\",\n   \"publisher\": \"zacks.com\",\n   \"publication_date\": \"2026-09-28\",\n   \"text\": \"In the latest trading session, Nvidia (NVDA) closed at $228.86, marking a +1.68% move from the previous day. The stock's change was more than the S&P 500's daily loss of 0.77%. Meanwhile, the Dow experienced a drop of 0.67%, and the technology-dominated Nasdaq saw a decrease of 0.92%.\\nShares of the maker of graphics chips for gaming and artificial intelligence have appreciated by 3.46% over the course of the past month, underperforming the Computer and Technology sector's gain of 6.26%, and outperforming the S&P 500's gain of 0.96%.\\nAnalysts and investors alike will be keeping a close eye on the performance of Nvidia in its upcoming earnings disclosure. The company's upcoming EPS is projected at $2.47, signifying a 90.00% increase compared to the same quarter of the previous year. Simultaneously, our latest consensus estimate expects the revenue to be $109.02 billion, showing a 91.24% escalation compared to the year-ago quarter.\\nFor the full year, the Zacks Consensus Estimates are projecting earnings of $9.25 per share and revenue of $406.05 billion, which would represent changes of +93.92% and +88.04%, respectively, from the prior year.\\nInvestors should also take note of any recent adjustments to analyst estimates for Nvidia. Recent revisions tend to reflect the latest near-term business trends. Consequently, upward revisions in estimates express analysts' positivity towards the business operations and its ability to generate profits.\\nBased on our research, we believe these estimate revisions are directly related to near-term stock moves. We developed the Zacks Rank to capitalize on this phenomenon. Our system takes these estimate changes into account and delivers a clear, actionable rating model.\\nThe Zacks Rank system, which varies between #1 (Strong Buy) and #5 (Strong Sell), carries an impressive track record of exceeding expectations, confirmed by external audits, with stocks at #1 delivering an average annual return of +25% since 1988. Over the past month, the Zacks Consensus EPS estimate has shifted 4.73% upward. Right now, Nvidia possesses a Zacks Rank of #1 (Strong Buy).\\nValuation is also important, so investors should note that Nvidia has a Forward P/E ratio of 24.34 right now. This represents a discount compared to its industry average Forward P/E of 38.49.\\nIt is also worth noting that NVDA currently has a PEG ratio of 1.74. The PEG ratio bears resemblance to the frequently used P/E ratio, but this parameter also includes the company's expected earnings growth trajectory. The average PEG ratio for the Semiconductor - General industry stood at 1.74 at the close of the market yesterday.\\nThe Semiconductor - General industry is part of the Computer and Technology sector. At present, this industry carries a Zacks Industry Rank of 49, placing it within the top 20% of over 250 industries.\\nThe Zacks Industry Rank gauges the strength of our industry groups by measuring the average Zacks Rank of the individual stocks within the groups. Our research shows that the top 50% rated industries outperform the bottom half by a factor of 2 to 1.\\nBe sure to use Zacks.com to monitor all these stock-influencing metrics, and more, throughout the forthcoming trading sessions.\",\n   \"named_figures\": [\n    {\n     \"figure\": 1,\n     \"text\": \"the upcoming EPS projected at $2.47\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"figure\": 2,\n     \"text\": \"the consensus estimate of $109.02 billion revenue for the quarter\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"figure\": 3,\n     \"text\": \"the Zacks Consensus Estimate of $406.05 billion revenue for the full year\"\n    }\n   ],\n   \"open_questions\": [\n    {\n     \"id\": \"clean-1\",\n     \"question\": \"What total of credit support and minimum-revenue guarantees to AI labs and cloud partners will NVIDIA disclose at its next annual report?\",\n     \"period\": \"Fiscal 2027 annual report (fiscal year ending late January 2027; Form 10-K expected around February 2027) — set as the next annual report following this 22 September 2026 item, per section 3 (obligation already undertaken tracked by its amount at the next annual report; the additional AI lab's credit support is not yet signed and is checked within 18 months).\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"id\": \"clean-2\",\n     \"question\": \"How much will NVIDIA disclose in purchase and supply commitments (obligations to buy capacity, components or services) at its next annual report?\",\n     \"period\": \"Fiscal 2027 annual report (fiscal year ending late January 2027; Form 10-K expected around February 2027) — the next annual report after this 22 September 2026 item, per section 3 (an obligation already undertaken is tracked by its amount at the next annual report).\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"id\": \"clean-3\",\n     \"question\": \"Will Nvidia be permitted to sell its current Blackwell-generation AI chips into China — through U.S. export approval and Chinese acceptance of shipments — by the end of fiscal 2027?\",\n     \"period\": \"NVIDIA fiscal year ending late January 2027 — the current fiscal year-end, used as the check date for a pending regulatory decision per section 3 (item gives no date of its own).\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"id\": \"clean-4\",\n     \"question\": \"Will NVIDIA's next-generation Vera Rubin AI platform, which the company says has entered full production, reach volume customer shipments (first revenue recognition) by the end of fiscal 2027?\",\n     \"period\": \"NVIDIA fiscal year ending late January 2027 (FY2027), checked at NVIDIA's quarterly results and FY2027 Form 10-K. The item gives no shipment date; the current fiscal year-end following the September 2026 report that Vera Rubin entered full production is used as the check date.\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"id\": \"clean-5\",\n     \"question\": \"How much will the five largest AI hyperscalers spend in total on data centers in calendar 2026?\",\n     \"period\": \"Calendar year 2026 (the item's 'this year' in a 23 September 2026 article), checked at the hyperscalers' Q4/full-year 2026 earnings and capex disclosures in early 2027.\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"id\": \"clean-6\",\n     \"question\": \"Will NVIDIA complete its approximately $12.9 billion acquisition of Hugging Face, agreed in 2026, by the check date?\",\n     \"period\": \"Check date 24 March 2028 (18 months after the 25 September 2026 item) — set per section 3 for a sized acquisition not yet completed, because the item gives no signing or closing date; also observable in NVIDIA's quarterly filings before then.\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"id\": \"clean-7\",\n     \"question\": \"Will China's Ministry of Industry and Information Technology formally approve purchases of Nvidia's new RTX Pro 5500 high-end GPU by Alibaba, ByteDance and other Chinese firms by the end of Nvidia's fiscal 2027, allowing those sales to proceed?\",\n     \"period\": \"NVIDIA fiscal year ending late January 2027 — the current fiscal year-end used as the check date for a pending regulatory decision per section 3, because the item gives no date of its own.\"\n    }\n   ]\n  },\n  {\n   \"article\": 10,\n   \"title\": \"When Should You Buy NVIDIA Stock After This Run?\",\n   \"publisher\": \"trefis.com\",\n   \"publication_date\": \"2026-09-28\",\n   \"text\": \"When Should You Buy NVIDIA Stock After This Run?\\nA $10,000 holding in NVIDIA (NVDA) stock three months ago was worth about $11,700 as of September 25, 2026. Yet against profits, the shares still trade near the low end of their ten-year range. If you have been waiting to buy NVIDIA, have you already missed it?\\nHave You Missed The Low Multiple On NVIDIA Stock?\\nNot if you judge it by earnings against its own past. The price-to-earnings ratio, or P/E, is the share price divided by a year of profit per share. NVIDIA trades at 28.1 times earnings , above the 22.1 for the S&P 500. Over the past ten years its P/E has ranged from 19.6 to 143.1, and today it is near the bottom.\\nBut a low P/E only stays low if profit holds up. NVIDIA’s operating margin, the share of revenue left after running costs, is 65%, its highest in ten years. Its three-year average is 59%. If margins slipped back toward that average, the same price would buy less profit.\\nCash looks less comfortable than earnings. Against operating cash flow, the cash the business brings in from running itself, the stock costs 40.4 times. The S&P 500 costs 14.5 times. Part of the cash has gone into inventory. On the fiscal Q2 2027 call, management said inventory rose to $32 billion as NVIDIA prepared for the Vera Rubin launch. On earnings or on cash, a price built on peak margins needs profit growth to continue.\\nHow Much Revenue Is NVIDIA Stock Counting On?\\nThe price appears to assume more revenue than NVIDIA itself has forecast for fiscal Q3 2027. Management guided revenue for that quarter to $108 billion, plus or minus 2%. The consensus forecast for that quarter is $111.3 billion, above the top of that range.\\nThat expectation has a record behind it. Management said fiscal Q2 2027 revenue of $96 billion more than doubled from a year earlier. Growth also sped up for the fourth quarter in a row.\\nThe newly launched Vera Rubin platform is one source of that revenue. Management expects it to make up about 20% of data center revenue in fiscal Q3 2027. Management says supply, not demand, is the limit. Management expects supply to remain a bottleneck at least through the end of fiscal 2028.\\nGross margin, the share of revenue left after making the product, is a different measure from operating margin. For fiscal Q3 2027 it is guided to 74%, plus or minus half a point, down from 75% in fiscal Q2 2027. Management pointed to higher component costs, especially memory. The next report has to beat NVIDIA’s own revenue forecast. The stock has not always taken its reports well.\\nNVIDIA’s Fiscal Q3 2027 Results Land in November\\nNVIDIA is expected to report on or around November 17, 2026. That report covers fiscal Q3 2027, and it will show whether revenue matches what the price appears to assume.\\nThe stock’s record after reports runs both ways. Over the last six reports, NVIDIA shares fell after four and rose after two, measured over two trading days. The biggest drop was 9.4%, after the February 25, 2026, report. The biggest gain was 3.8%, after the August 26, 2026, report.\\nA broad market sell-off is a separate risk. During the 2025 tariff shock, NVIDIA stock fell 32% from peak to low, against 19% for the S&P 500.\\nIf fiscal Q3 2027 revenue lands below $111.3 billion, the price will have asked for more than NVIDIA delivered. Buying now would then prove early. Suppose revenue clears $111.3 billion and gross margin comes in at 74% or better. The business will then have kept pace with the run. Buying at today’s price would not have been too late.\",\n   \"named_figures\": [\n    {\n     \"figure\": 1,\n     \"text\": \"the $111.3 billion consensus forecast for the quarter\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"figure\": 2,\n     \"text\": \"the $108 billion revenue guided for the quarter\"\n    }\n   ],\n   \"open_questions\": [\n    {\n     \"id\": \"clean-1\",\n     \"question\": \"What total of credit support and minimum-revenue guarantees to AI labs and cloud partners will NVIDIA disclose at its next annual report?\",\n     \"period\": \"Fiscal 2027 annual report (fiscal year ending late January 2027; Form 10-K expected around February 2027) — set as the next annual report following this 22 September 2026 item, per section 3 (obligation already undertaken tracked by its amount at the next annual report; the additional AI lab's credit support is not yet signed and is checked within 18 months).\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"id\": \"clean-2\",\n     \"question\": \"How much will NVIDIA disclose in purchase and supply commitments (obligations to buy capacity, components or services) at its next annual report?\",\n     \"period\": \"Fiscal 2027 annual report (fiscal year ending late January 2027; Form 10-K expected around February 2027) — the next annual report after this 22 September 2026 item, per section 3 (an obligation already undertaken is tracked by its amount at the next annual report).\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"id\": \"clean-3\",\n     \"question\": \"Will Nvidia be permitted to sell its current Blackwell-generation AI chips into China — through U.S. export approval and Chinese acceptance of shipments — by the end of fiscal 2027?\",\n     \"period\": \"NVIDIA fiscal year ending late January 2027 — the current fiscal year-end, used as the check date for a pending regulatory decision per section 3 (item gives no date of its own).\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"id\": \"clean-4\",\n     \"question\": \"Will NVIDIA's next-generation Vera Rubin AI platform, which the company says has entered full production, reach volume customer shipments (first revenue recognition) by the end of fiscal 2027?\",\n     \"period\": \"NVIDIA fiscal year ending late January 2027 (FY2027), checked at NVIDIA's quarterly results and FY2027 Form 10-K. The item gives no shipment date; the current fiscal year-end following the September 2026 report that Vera Rubin entered full production is used as the check date.\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"id\": \"clean-5\",\n     \"question\": \"How much will the five largest AI hyperscalers spend in total on data centers in calendar 2026?\",\n     \"period\": \"Calendar year 2026 (the item's 'this year' in a 23 September 2026 article), checked at the hyperscalers' Q4/full-year 2026 earnings and capex disclosures in early 2027.\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"id\": \"clean-6\",\n     \"question\": \"Will NVIDIA complete its approximately $12.9 billion acquisition of Hugging Face, agreed in 2026, by the check date?\",\n     \"period\": \"Check date 24 March 2028 (18 months after the 25 September 2026 item) — set per section 3 for a sized acquisition not yet completed, because the item gives no signing or closing date; also observable in NVIDIA's quarterly filings before then.\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"id\": \"clean-7\",\n     \"question\": \"Will China's Ministry of Industry and Information Technology formally approve purchases of Nvidia's new RTX Pro 5500 high-end GPU by Alibaba, ByteDance and other Chinese firms by the end of Nvidia's fiscal 2027, allowing those sales to proceed?\",\n     \"period\": \"NVIDIA fiscal year ending late January 2027 — the current fiscal year-end used as the check date for a pending regulatory decision per section 3, because the item gives no date of its own.\"\n    }\n   ]\n  }\n ],\n \"answers\": [\n  {\n   \"answer\": \"B24\",\n   \"article\": 2,\n   \"statements_with_figures\": [\n    {\n     \"id\": \"e1\",\n     \"summary\": \"Nvidia's revenue and net income are each expected to grow by roughly 90% or more in its current fiscal year, according to estimates reported by Bloomberg, even as the stock trades near its lowest valuation in more than a decade.\",\n     \"passages\": [\n      \"Nvidia $NVDA stock is trading near its lowest valuation in more than a decade, according to Bloomberg , even as the chipmaker's revenue and net income are each expected to grow by roughly 90% or more in its current fiscal year.\"\n     ],\n     \"statement_party\": \"Bloomberg (compiled estimates)\",\n     \"statement_date\": null,\n     \"figures\": [\n      {\n       \"n\": 1,\n       \"measure\": \"Revenue growth\",\n       \"period\": \"Current fiscal year\",\n       \"value\": \"roughly 90% or more\",\n       \"kind\": \"estimate\",\n       \"basis\": \"Company-level revenue growth expected, per Bloomberg\",\n       \"party\": \"Bloomberg (compiled estimates)\"\n      },\n      {\n       \"n\": 2,\n       \"measure\": \"Net income growth\",\n       \"period\": \"Current fiscal year\",\n       \"value\": \"roughly 90% or more\",\n       \"kind\": \"estimate\",\n       \"basis\": \"Company-level net income growth expected, per Bloomberg\",\n       \"party\": \"Bloomberg (compiled estimates)\"\n      }\n     ],\n     \"links\": \"none\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"id\": \"e2\",\n     \"summary\": \"Nvidia's gross margin came in at 75% in the last reported quarter, and analyst estimates compiled by Bloomberg show it sliding under 72% by the fourth quarter, with a recovery expected thereafter; rising costs for key components such as memory chips are cited as a central factor.\",\n     \"passages\": [\n      \"One drag on the stock's valuation is a squeeze on profitability. Nvidia 's gross margin came in at 75% last quarter but analyst estimates compiled by Bloomberg show it sliding under 72% by the fourth quarter, with a recovery expected thereafter. Rising costs for key components such as memory chips are a central factor.\"\n     ],\n     \"statement_party\": \"Nvidia (reported gross margin); Bloomberg-compiled analyst estimates\",\n     \"statement_date\": null,\n     \"figures\": [\n      {\n       \"n\": 1,\n       \"measure\": \"Gross margin\",\n       \"period\": \"Last quarter\",\n       \"value\": \"75%\",\n       \"kind\": \"actual\",\n       \"basis\": \"Company-level gross margin, as reported\",\n       \"party\": \"Nvidia (reported gross margin); Bloomberg-compiled analyst estimates\"\n      },\n      {\n       \"n\": 2,\n       \"measure\": \"Gross margin\",\n       \"period\": \"Fourth quarter\",\n       \"value\": \"under 72%\",\n       \"kind\": \"estimate\",\n       \"basis\": \"Analyst estimates compiled by Bloomberg\",\n       \"party\": \"Nvidia (reported gross margin); Bloomberg-compiled analyst estimates\"\n      }\n     ],\n     \"links\": \"none\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"id\": \"e3\",\n     \"summary\": \"Nvidia reported second-quarter revenue that more than doubled year over year and projected 70% revenue growth for fiscal 2028, well above the 45% analysts had expected at the time; the stock rose roughly 7% after the earnings report.\",\n     \"passages\": [\n      \"The valuation discount follows a stretch of strong results. Nvidia stock climbed roughly 7% after its second-quarter earnings , when revenue more than doubled and the company projected 70% revenue growth in fiscal 2028 — well above the 45% analysts had expected at the time.\"\n     ],\n     \"statement_party\": \"Nvidia (results and guidance); analysts (prior expectation)\",\n     \"statement_date\": null,\n     \"figures\": [\n      {\n       \"n\": 1,\n       \"measure\": \"Revenue growth\",\n       \"period\": \"Second quarter\",\n       \"value\": \"more than doubled\",\n       \"kind\": \"actual\",\n       \"basis\": \"Year-over-year company revenue growth, as reported\",\n       \"party\": \"Nvidia (results and guidance); analysts (prior expectation)\"\n      },\n      {\n       \"n\": 2,\n       \"measure\": \"Revenue growth guidance\",\n       \"period\": \"Fiscal 2028\",\n       \"value\": \"70%\",\n       \"kind\": \"guidance\",\n       \"basis\": \"Company projection, per the article\",\n       \"party\": \"Nvidia (results and guidance); analysts (prior expectation)\"\n      },\n      {\n       \"n\": 3,\n       \"measure\": \"Revenue growth expectation\",\n       \"period\": \"Fiscal 2028\",\n       \"value\": \"45%\",\n       \"kind\": \"estimate\",\n       \"basis\": \"What analysts had expected at the time, per the article\",\n       \"party\": \"Nvidia (results and guidance); analysts (prior expectation)\"\n      }\n     ],\n     \"links\": \"none\"\n    }\n   ]\n  },\n  {\n   \"answer\": \"B27\",\n   \"article\": 7,\n   \"statements_with_figures\": [\n    {\n     \"id\": \"ev-1\",\n     \"summary\": \"NVIDIA management expects Vera Rubin, its newest platform, to be the fastest product ramp in the company's history, and sizes NVIDIA's revenue opportunity at about $40 billion per gigawatt of capacity with Vera Rubin, against $25 billion with Blackwell.\",\n     \"passages\": [\n      \"NVIDIA expects Vera Rubin, its newest platform, to be the fastest product ramp in the company’s history. Management puts NVIDIA’s revenue opportunity at about $40 billion per gigawatt of capacity with Vera Rubin, against $25 billion with Blackwell.\"\n     ],\n     \"statement_party\": \"NVIDIA management\",\n     \"statement_date\": null,\n     \"figures\": [\n      {\n       \"n\": 1,\n       \"measure\": \"revenue opportunity per gigawatt of capacity with Vera Rubin\",\n       \"period\": null,\n       \"value\": \"about $40 billion per gigawatt\",\n       \"kind\": \"estimate\",\n       \"basis\": \"management's sizing, compared with $25 billion per gigawatt with Blackwell\",\n       \"party\": \"NVIDIA management\"\n      }\n     ],\n     \"links\": [\n      {\n       \"question\": \"clean-4\",\n       \"new_question\": false,\n       \"reason\": \"Management's expectation that Vera Rubin is the fastest ramp in company history and its revenue intensity per gigawatt ($40 billion vs $25 billion for Blackwell) bears on the scale and timing of the Vera Rubin ramp the thesis tracks, though the item gives no shipment date or confirmation of volume customer shipments or first revenue recognition.\"\n      }\n     ]\n    },\n    {\n     \"id\": \"ev-3\",\n     \"summary\": \"NVIDIA management guided to about 70% revenue growth for fiscal 2028 — the first time it has guided a full year ahead — with the CEO saying demand is much greater than 70% and that supply is what holds the guide there.\",\n     \"passages\": [\n      \"For fiscal 2028, management has guided about 70% revenue growth, the first time it has guided a full year ahead. That guide does not depend on finding more demand. The CEO says demand is much greater than 70%, and that supply is what holds the guide there.\"\n     ],\n     \"statement_party\": \"NVIDIA management (CEO)\",\n     \"statement_date\": null,\n     \"figures\": [\n      {\n       \"n\": 1,\n       \"measure\": \"revenue growth\",\n       \"period\": \"fiscal 2028\",\n       \"value\": \"about 70%\",\n       \"kind\": \"guidance\",\n       \"basis\": \"company's first full-year-ahead guide; management says supply, not demand, holds the guide there\",\n       \"party\": \"NVIDIA management (CEO)\"\n      }\n     ],\n     \"links\": \"none\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"id\": \"ev-5\",\n     \"summary\": \"NVIDIA has invested nearly $50 billion in frontier AI labs, part of a demand picture the article says leans on NVIDIA's own balance sheet; management concedes some will call this circular financing and says the compute can be redeployed to other customers.\",\n     \"passages\": [\n      \"Some of that demand also leans on NVIDIA’s own balance sheet. NVIDIA has invested nearly $50 billion in frontier AI labs. Management concedes some will call this circular financing and says the compute can be redeployed to other customers.\"\n     ],\n     \"statement_party\": \"NVIDIA\",\n     \"statement_date\": null,\n     \"figures\": [\n      {\n       \"n\": 1,\n       \"measure\": \"investment in frontier AI labs\",\n       \"period\": null,\n       \"value\": \"nearly $50 billion\",\n       \"kind\": \"actual\",\n       \"basis\": \"cumulative amount invested by NVIDIA, as reported by the article\",\n       \"party\": \"NVIDIA\"\n      }\n     ],\n     \"links\": [\n      {\n       \"question\": \"clean-1\",\n       \"new_question\": false,\n       \"reason\": \"Reports the equity-investment side of the comparison the thesis tracks — nearly $50 billion invested in frontier AI labs — and management's response to 'circular financing' criticism; the item gives no guarantee, credit-support or revenue-backstop amount.\"\n      }\n     ]\n    },\n    {\n     \"id\": \"ev-6\",\n     \"summary\": \"NVIDIA guided its gross margin lower, to settle in the 72% to 73% range in fiscal 2028, as memory prices rose more than management expected, partly offset by price increases NVIDIA has already executed.\",\n     \"passages\": [\n      \"Gross margin is guided lower. Memory prices have risen more than management expected, and the guide has that margin settling in the 72% to 73% range in fiscal 2028, helped by price increases NVIDIA has already executed.\"\n     ],\n     \"statement_party\": \"NVIDIA management\",\n     \"statement_date\": null,\n     \"figures\": [\n      {\n       \"n\": 1,\n       \"measure\": \"gross margin\",\n       \"period\": \"fiscal 2028\",\n       \"value\": \"72% to 73%\",\n       \"kind\": \"guidance\",\n       \"basis\": \"guided range; memory prices higher than management expected, helped by price increases already executed (GAAP/non-GAAP basis not stated)\",\n       \"party\": \"NVIDIA management\"\n      }\n     ],\n     \"links\": \"none\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"id\": \"ev-7\",\n     \"summary\": \"The 33 analyst estimates of NVIDIA's fiscal 2028 earnings span $12.62 to $17.49 a share, a wide band for the year on which its forward multiple is priced.\",\n     \"passages\": [\n      \"Analysts also disagree on what that growth earns. The 33 estimates of fiscal 2028 earnings run from $12.62 to $17.49 a share, a wide band for the year the forward multiple is priced on.\"\n     ],\n     \"statement_party\": \"33 unnamed analyst estimates (as reported by Trefis)\",\n     \"statement_date\": null,\n     \"figures\": [\n      {\n       \"n\": 1,\n       \"measure\": \"EPS estimates (range across 33 analysts)\",\n       \"period\": \"fiscal 2028\",\n       \"value\": \"$12.62 to $17.49 a share\",\n       \"kind\": \"estimate\",\n       \"basis\": \"range of 33 analyst estimates\",\n       \"party\": \"33 unnamed analyst estimates (as reported by Trefis)\"\n      }\n     ],\n     \"links\": \"none\"\n    }\n   ]\n  },\n  {\n   \"answer\": \"B29\",\n   \"article\": 2,\n   \"statements_with_figures\": [\n    {\n     \"id\": \"e1\",\n     \"summary\": \"Quartz, citing Bloomberg-compiled expectations, reports that NVIDIA's revenue and net income are each expected to grow by roughly 90% or more in its current fiscal year, even as the stock trades below 17 times forward earnings.\",\n     \"passages\": [\n      \"Nvidia $NVDA stock is trading near its lowest valuation in more than a decade, according to Bloomberg , even as the chipmaker's revenue and net income are each expected to grow by roughly 90% or more in its current fiscal year.\"\n     ],\n     \"statement_party\": \"Bloomberg (compiled analyst estimates)\",\n     \"statement_date\": null,\n     \"figures\": [\n      {\n       \"n\": 1,\n       \"measure\": \"revenue growth\",\n       \"period\": \"current fiscal year (FY2027)\",\n       \"value\": \"roughly 90% or more\",\n       \"kind\": \"estimate\",\n       \"basis\": \"consensus expectation compiled by Bloomberg, per Quartz\",\n       \"party\": \"Bloomberg (compiled analyst estimates)\"\n      },\n      {\n       \"n\": 2,\n       \"measure\": \"net income growth\",\n       \"period\": \"current fiscal year (FY2027)\",\n       \"value\": \"roughly 90% or more\",\n       \"kind\": \"estimate\",\n       \"basis\": \"consensus expectation compiled by Bloomberg, per Quartz\",\n       \"party\": \"Bloomberg (compiled analyst estimates)\"\n      }\n     ],\n     \"links\": \"none\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"id\": \"e2\",\n     \"summary\": \"At NVIDIA's fiscal second-quarter earnings (reported before 22 September 2026), the company's revenue more than doubled year over year and NVIDIA projected 70% revenue growth for fiscal 2028, well above the 45% analysts had expected at the time.\",\n     \"passages\": [\n      \"The valuation discount follows a stretch of strong results. Nvidia stock climbed roughly 7% after its second-quarter earnings , when revenue more than doubled and the company projected 70% revenue growth in fiscal 2028 — well above the 45% analysts had expected at the time.\"\n     ],\n     \"statement_party\": \"NVIDIA\",\n     \"statement_date\": null,\n     \"figures\": [\n      {\n       \"n\": 1,\n       \"measure\": \"revenue growth\",\n       \"period\": \"fiscal second quarter (FY2027)\",\n       \"value\": \"more than doubled\",\n       \"kind\": \"actual\",\n       \"basis\": \"year-over-year, as reported by Quartz\",\n       \"party\": \"NVIDIA\"\n      },\n      {\n       \"n\": 2,\n       \"measure\": \"revenue growth guidance\",\n       \"period\": \"fiscal 2028\",\n       \"value\": \"70%\",\n       \"kind\": \"guidance\",\n       \"basis\": \"company projection made at the fiscal Q2 earnings\",\n       \"party\": \"NVIDIA\"\n      },\n      {\n       \"n\": 3,\n       \"measure\": \"analyst revenue growth expectation\",\n       \"period\": \"fiscal 2028\",\n       \"value\": \"45%\",\n       \"kind\": \"estimate\",\n       \"basis\": \"analysts' expectation at the time of the Q2 earnings, per Quartz\",\n       \"party\": \"NVIDIA\"\n      }\n     ],\n     \"links\": \"none\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"id\": \"e3\",\n     \"summary\": \"NVIDIA's gross margin came in at 75% in its last reported quarter (fiscal Q2, before 22 September 2026), according to Quartz.\",\n     \"passages\": [\n      \"Nvidia 's gross margin came in at 75% last quarter\"\n     ],\n     \"statement_party\": \"NVIDIA\",\n     \"statement_date\": null,\n     \"figures\": [\n      {\n       \"n\": 1,\n       \"measure\": \"gross margin\",\n       \"period\": \"last quarter (fiscal Q2 FY2027)\",\n       \"value\": \"75%\",\n       \"kind\": \"actual\",\n       \"basis\": null,\n       \"party\": \"NVIDIA\"\n      }\n     ],\n     \"links\": \"none\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"id\": \"e4\",\n     \"summary\": \"Analyst estimates compiled by Bloomberg, reported by Quartz, show NVIDIA's gross margin sliding from 75% to under 72% by the fourth quarter, with a recovery expected thereafter; rising costs for key components such as memory chips are described as a central factor.\",\n     \"passages\": [\n      \"but analyst estimates compiled by Bloomberg show it sliding under 72% by the fourth quarter, with a recovery expected thereafter.\",\n      \"Rising costs for key components such as memory chips are a central factor.\"\n     ],\n     \"statement_party\": \"Bloomberg (compiled analyst estimates)\",\n     \"statement_date\": null,\n     \"figures\": [\n      {\n       \"n\": 1,\n       \"measure\": \"gross margin\",\n       \"period\": \"fourth quarter (FY2027)\",\n       \"value\": \"under 72%\",\n       \"kind\": \"estimate\",\n       \"basis\": \"analyst estimates compiled by Bloomberg\",\n       \"party\": \"Bloomberg (compiled analyst estimates)\"\n      }\n     ],\n     \"links\": [\n      {\n       \"question\": \"clean-2\",\n       \"new_question\": false,\n       \"reason\": \"Rising costs for key components such as memory chips are the stated driver of the expected margin squeeze; this bears on the input-cost side of the purchase and supply commitments whose disclosed dollar amount clean-2 tracks. It is an input-cost signal only: the item gives no commitment, volume or purchase-obligation figures.\"\n      }\n     ]\n    }\n   ]\n  },\n  {\n   \"answer\": \"B33\",\n   \"article\": 8,\n   \"statements_with_figures\": [\n    {\n     \"id\": \"e1\",\n     \"summary\": \"NVIDIA's fiscal 2026 (year ended January 2026) revenue reached nearly $215.9 billion, up roughly 65.5% year over year, producing net income of approximately $120.1 billion and a net margin of approximately 55.6%, consistent with the 55.8% net margin of the prior fiscal year, according to Fool.com's comparison article.\",\n     \"passages\": [\n      \"In fiscal 2026, which ended in January 2026, revenue reached nearly $215.9 billion, up roughly 65.5% year over year. This scale produced net income of approximately $120.1 billion. The net margin remained remarkably high at approximately 55.6%, consistent with the 55.8% net margin achieved in the previous fiscal year.\"\n     ],\n     \"statement_party\": \"NVIDIA\",\n     \"statement_date\": null,\n     \"figures\": [\n      {\n       \"n\": 1,\n       \"measure\": \"revenue\",\n       \"period\": \"fiscal 2026 (ended January 2026)\",\n       \"value\": \"nearly $215.9 billion\",\n       \"kind\": \"actual\",\n       \"basis\": \"company total revenue, fiscal year\",\n       \"party\": \"NVIDIA\"\n      },\n      {\n       \"n\": 2,\n       \"measure\": \"revenue growth year over year\",\n       \"period\": \"fiscal 2026\",\n       \"value\": \"roughly 65.5%\",\n       \"kind\": \"actual\",\n       \"basis\": \"company total revenue, fiscal year\",\n       \"party\": \"NVIDIA\"\n      },\n      {\n       \"n\": 3,\n       \"measure\": \"net income\",\n       \"period\": \"fiscal 2026\",\n       \"value\": \"approximately $120.1 billion\",\n       \"kind\": \"actual\",\n       \"basis\": \"company total, fiscal year\",\n       \"party\": \"NVIDIA\"\n      },\n      {\n       \"n\": 4,\n       \"measure\": \"net margin\",\n       \"period\": \"fiscal 2026\",\n       \"value\": \"approximately 55.6%\",\n       \"kind\": \"actual\",\n       \"basis\": \"company total; prior fiscal year 55.8%\",\n       \"party\": \"NVIDIA\"\n      }\n     ]\n    },\n    {\n     \"id\": \"e2\",\n     \"summary\": \"NVIDIA's total revenue more than doubled year over year in its latest reported quarter, with almost all of it coming from data center customers, and its data center business generated $89 billion in that quarter alone, per Fool.com.\",\n     \"passages\": [\n      \"Revenue in its latest quarter also more than doubled, with almost all of it coming from data center customers, and management's outlook for the current quarter assumes no data center compute sales to China at all.\",\n      \"Nvidia's data center business brought in $89 billion in its latest quarter alone, and management guided the next quarter to $108 billion, excluding any China compute sales.\"\n     ],\n     \"statement_party\": \"NVIDIA\",\n     \"statement_date\": null,\n     \"figures\": [\n      {\n       \"n\": 1,\n       \"measure\": \"data center revenue\",\n       \"period\": \"latest reported quarter (as of 25 September 2026 article)\",\n       \"value\": \"$89 billion\",\n       \"kind\": \"actual\",\n       \"basis\": \"data center business\",\n       \"party\": \"NVIDIA\"\n      }\n     ]\n    },\n    {\n     \"id\": \"e3\",\n     \"summary\": \"NVIDIA management guided its next quarter to $108 billion of revenue, with an outlook that assumes no data center compute sales to China at all (excluding any China compute sales), as reported by Fool.com.\",\n     \"passages\": [\n      \"Nvidia's data center business brought in $89 billion in its latest quarter alone, and management guided the next quarter to $108 billion, excluding any China compute sales.\",\n      \"Revenue in its latest quarter also more than doubled, with almost all of it coming from data center customers, and management's outlook for the current quarter assumes no data center compute sales to China at all.\"\n     ],\n     \"statement_party\": \"NVIDIA\",\n     \"statement_date\": null,\n     \"figures\": [\n      {\n       \"n\": 1,\n       \"measure\": \"revenue guidance\",\n       \"period\": \"next quarter (quarter following the latest reported quarter)\",\n       \"value\": \"$108 billion\",\n       \"kind\": \"guidance\",\n       \"basis\": \"company outlook, excluding any China compute sales\",\n       \"party\": \"NVIDIA\"\n      }\n     ]\n    },\n    {\n     \"id\": \"e4\",\n     \"summary\": \"NVIDIA recently agreed to acquire Hugging Face for approximately $12.9 billion to expand its software capabilities, as reported by Fool.com; the item does not say the transaction has closed.\",\n     \"passages\": [\n      \"The company also recently agreed to acquire Hugging Face for approximately $12.9 billion to expand its software capabilities.\"\n     ],\n     \"statement_party\": \"NVIDIA\",\n     \"statement_date\": \"recently\",\n     \"figures\": [\n      {\n       \"n\": 1,\n       \"measure\": \"acquisition consideration for Hugging Face\",\n       \"period\": null,\n       \"value\": \"approximately $12.9 billion\",\n       \"kind\": \"actual\",\n       \"basis\": \"agreed acquisition, not stated as completed\",\n       \"party\": \"NVIDIA\"\n      }\n     ]\n    },\n    {\n     \"id\": \"e5\",\n     \"summary\": \"NVIDIA disclosed that sales to two direct customers represented 22% and 14% of total revenue in fiscal 2026, respectively, a customer concentration the article flags as a risk.\",\n     \"passages\": [\n      \"Nvidia relies on a concentrated customer base, with sales to two direct customers representing 22% and 14% of total revenue in fiscal 2026, respectively.\"\n     ],\n     \"statement_party\": \"NVIDIA\",\n     \"statement_date\": null,\n     \"figures\": [\n      {\n       \"n\": 1,\n       \"measure\": \"sales to largest direct customer as share of total revenue\",\n       \"period\": \"fiscal 2026\",\n       \"value\": \"22%\",\n       \"kind\": \"actual\",\n       \"basis\": \"direct customer, company total revenue\",\n       \"party\": \"NVIDIA\"\n      },\n      {\n       \"n\": 2,\n       \"measure\": \"sales to second direct customer as share of total revenue\",\n       \"period\": \"fiscal 2026\",\n       \"value\": \"14%\",\n       \"kind\": \"actual\",\n       \"basis\": \"direct customer, company total revenue\",\n       \"party\": \"NVIDIA\"\n      }\n     ]\n    }\n   ]\n  },\n  {\n   \"answer\": \"B40\",\n   \"article\": 10,\n   \"statements_with_figures\": [\n    {\n     \"id\": \"ev-1\",\n     \"summary\": \"On NVIDIA's fiscal Q2 2027 earnings call, management said inventory rose to $32 billion as the company prepared for the Vera Rubin launch.\",\n     \"passages\": [\n      \"Part of the cash has gone into inventory. On the fiscal Q2 2027 call, management said inventory rose to $32 billion as NVIDIA prepared for the Vera Rubin launch.\"\n     ],\n     \"statement_party\": \"NVIDIA\",\n     \"statement_date\": null,\n     \"figures\": [\n      {\n       \"n\": 1,\n       \"measure\": \"inventory\",\n       \"period\": \"fiscal Q2 2027\",\n       \"value\": \"$32 billion\",\n       \"kind\": \"actual\",\n       \"basis\": null,\n       \"party\": \"NVIDIA\"\n      }\n     ],\n     \"links\": [\n      {\n       \"question\": \"clean-4\",\n       \"new_question\": false,\n       \"reason\": \"A $32 billion inventory build made in preparation for the Vera Rubin launch is a supply-side signal on how far the ramp toward volume shipments of Vera Rubin has progressed; it does not itself confirm shipments or revenue recognition.\"\n      }\n     ]\n    },\n    {\n     \"id\": \"ev-2\",\n     \"summary\": \"NVIDIA management guided fiscal Q3 2027 revenue to $108 billion, plus or minus 2%, while the consensus forecast for that quarter is $111.3 billion, above the top of that guidance range.\",\n     \"passages\": [\n      \"The price appears to assume more revenue than NVIDIA itself has forecast for fiscal Q3 2027. Management guided revenue for that quarter to $108 billion, plus or minus 2%. The consensus forecast for that quarter is $111.3 billion, above the top of that range.\"\n     ],\n     \"statement_party\": \"NVIDIA (guidance); consensus estimate as reported by Trefis\",\n     \"statement_date\": null,\n     \"figures\": [\n      {\n       \"n\": 1,\n       \"measure\": \"revenue guidance\",\n       \"period\": \"fiscal Q3 2027\",\n       \"value\": \"$108 billion\",\n       \"kind\": \"guidance\",\n       \"basis\": \"plus or minus 2%\",\n       \"party\": \"NVIDIA (guidance); consensus estimate as reported by Trefis\"\n      },\n      {\n       \"n\": 2,\n       \"measure\": \"consensus revenue forecast\",\n       \"period\": \"fiscal Q3 2027\",\n       \"value\": \"$111.3 billion\",\n       \"kind\": \"estimate\",\n       \"basis\": \"consensus forecast, above the top of NVIDIA's guidance range\",\n       \"party\": \"NVIDIA (guidance); consensus estimate as reported by Trefis\"\n      }\n     ],\n     \"links\": \"none\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"id\": \"ev-3\",\n     \"summary\": \"NVIDIA management said fiscal Q2 2027 revenue was $96 billion, more than double the year-earlier figure, with growth accelerating for the fourth consecutive quarter.\",\n     \"passages\": [\n      \"That expectation has a record behind it. Management said fiscal Q2 2027 revenue of $96 billion more than doubled from a year earlier. Growth also sped up for the fourth quarter in a row.\"\n     ],\n     \"statement_party\": \"NVIDIA\",\n     \"statement_date\": null,\n     \"figures\": [\n      {\n       \"n\": 1,\n       \"measure\": \"revenue\",\n       \"period\": \"fiscal Q2 2027\",\n       \"value\": \"$96 billion\",\n       \"kind\": \"actual\",\n       \"basis\": \"more than doubled from a year earlier; growth accelerated for the fourth consecutive quarter\",\n       \"party\": \"NVIDIA\"\n      }\n     ],\n     \"links\": \"none\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"id\": \"ev-4\",\n     \"summary\": \"NVIDIA guided gross margin for fiscal Q3 2027 to 74%, plus or minus half a point, down from 75% in fiscal Q2 2027, with management pointing to higher component costs, especially memory.\",\n     \"passages\": [\n      \"Gross margin, the share of revenue left after making the product, is a different measure from operating margin. For fiscal Q3 2027 it is guided to 74%, plus or minus half a point, down from 75% in fiscal Q2 2027. Management pointed to higher component costs, especially memory.\"\n     ],\n     \"statement_party\": \"NVIDIA\",\n     \"statement_date\": null,\n     \"figures\": [\n      {\n       \"n\": 1,\n       \"measure\": \"gross margin guidance\",\n       \"period\": \"fiscal Q3 2027\",\n       \"value\": \"74%\",\n       \"kind\": \"guidance\",\n       \"basis\": \"plus or minus half a point; attributed to higher component costs, especially memory\",\n       \"party\": \"NVIDIA\"\n      },\n      {\n       \"n\": 2,\n       \"measure\": \"gross margin\",\n       \"period\": \"fiscal Q2 2027\",\n       \"value\": \"75%\",\n       \"kind\": \"actual\",\n       \"basis\": null,\n       \"party\": \"NVIDIA\"\n      }\n     ],\n     \"links\": \"none\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"id\": \"ev-5\",\n     \"summary\": \"NVIDIA management expects the newly launched Vera Rubin platform to make up about 20% of its data center revenue in fiscal Q3 2027.\",\n     \"passages\": [\n      \"The newly launched Vera Rubin platform is one source of that revenue. Management expects it to make up about 20% of data center revenue in fiscal Q3 2027.\"\n     ],\n     \"statement_party\": \"NVIDIA\",\n     \"statement_date\": null,\n     \"figures\": [\n      {\n       \"n\": 1,\n       \"measure\": \"Vera Rubin share of data center revenue\",\n       \"period\": \"fiscal Q3 2027\",\n       \"value\": \"about 20%\",\n       \"kind\": \"guidance\",\n       \"basis\": \"of data center revenue; management expectation\",\n       \"party\": \"NVIDIA\"\n      }\n     ],\n     \"links\": [\n      {\n       \"question\": \"clean-4\",\n       \"new_question\": false,\n       \"reason\": \"Management's expectation that Vera Rubin will already contribute about 20% of data center revenue in fiscal Q3 2027 bears on whether the platform reaches volume customer shipments and first revenue recognition within fiscal 2027; it is a forecast, not confirmed shipment or revenue.\"\n      }\n     ]\n    },\n    {\n     \"id\": \"ev-7\",\n     \"summary\": \"Trefis reports that NVIDIA's operating margin is 65%, its highest in ten years, against a three-year average of 59%.\",\n     \"passages\": [\n      \"But a low P/E only stays low if profit holds up. NVIDIA’s operating margin, the share of revenue left after running costs, is 65%, its highest in ten years. Its three-year average is 59%.\"\n     ],\n     \"statement_party\": \"Trefis (analysis of NVIDIA reported figures)\",\n     \"statement_date\": null,\n     \"figures\": [\n      {\n       \"n\": 1,\n       \"measure\": \"operating margin\",\n       \"period\": null,\n       \"value\": \"65%\",\n       \"kind\": \"actual\",\n       \"basis\": \"highest in ten years\",\n       \"party\": \"Trefis (analysis of NVIDIA reported figures)\"\n      },\n      {\n       \"n\": 2,\n       \"measure\": \"operating margin, three-year average\",\n       \"period\": \"trailing three years\",\n       \"value\": \"59%\",\n       \"kind\": \"actual\",\n       \"basis\": \"three-year average\",\n       \"party\": \"Trefis (analysis of NVIDIA reported figures)\"\n      }\n     ],\n     \"links\": \"none\"\n    }\n   ]\n  },\n  {\n   \"answer\": \"B43\",\n   \"article\": 10,\n   \"statements_with_figures\": [\n    {\n     \"id\": \"e1\",\n     \"summary\": \"NVIDIA management guided revenue for fiscal Q3 2027 to $108 billion, plus or minus 2%.\",\n     \"passages\": [\n      \"The price appears to assume more revenue than NVIDIA itself has forecast for fiscal Q3 2027. Management guided revenue for that quarter to $108 billion, plus or minus 2%.\"\n     ],\n     \"statement_party\": \"NVIDIA\",\n     \"statement_date\": null,\n     \"figures\": [\n      {\n       \"n\": 1,\n       \"measure\": \"revenue guidance\",\n       \"period\": \"fiscal Q3 2027\",\n       \"value\": \"$108 billion\",\n       \"kind\": \"guidance\",\n       \"basis\": \"plus or minus 2%\",\n       \"party\": \"NVIDIA\"\n      }\n     ],\n     \"links\": \"none\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"id\": \"e2\",\n     \"summary\": \"The consensus forecast for NVIDIA's fiscal Q3 2027 revenue is $111.3 billion, above the top of the company's own guidance range for that quarter.\",\n     \"passages\": [\n      \"The consensus forecast for that quarter is $111.3 billion, above the top of that range.\"\n     ],\n     \"statement_party\": null,\n     \"statement_date\": null,\n     \"figures\": [\n      {\n       \"n\": 1,\n       \"measure\": \"revenue consensus forecast\",\n       \"period\": \"fiscal Q3 2027\",\n       \"value\": \"$111.3 billion\",\n       \"kind\": \"estimate\",\n       \"basis\": null,\n       \"party\": \"consensus\"\n      }\n     ],\n     \"links\": \"none\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"id\": \"e3\",\n     \"summary\": \"NVIDIA management said fiscal Q2 2027 revenue was $96 billion, more than double the year-earlier figure, with growth accelerating for a fourth consecutive quarter.\",\n     \"passages\": [\n      \"That expectation has a record behind it. Management said fiscal Q2 2027 revenue of $96 billion more than doubled from a year earlier. Growth also sped up for the fourth quarter in a row.\"\n     ],\n     \"statement_party\": \"NVIDIA\",\n     \"statement_date\": null,\n     \"figures\": [\n      {\n       \"n\": 1,\n       \"measure\": \"revenue\",\n       \"period\": \"fiscal Q2 2027\",\n       \"value\": \"$96 billion\",\n       \"kind\": \"actual\",\n       \"basis\": null,\n       \"party\": \"NVIDIA\"\n      }\n     ],\n     \"links\": \"none\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"id\": \"e4\",\n     \"summary\": \"On the fiscal Q2 2027 call, NVIDIA management said inventory rose to $32 billion as the company prepared for the Vera Rubin launch.\",\n     \"passages\": [\n      \"Part of the cash has gone into inventory. On the fiscal Q2 2027 call, management said inventory rose to $32 billion as NVIDIA prepared for the Vera Rubin launch.\"\n     ],\n     \"statement_party\": \"NVIDIA\",\n     \"statement_date\": null,\n     \"figures\": [\n      {\n       \"n\": 1,\n       \"measure\": \"inventory\",\n       \"period\": \"fiscal Q2 2027\",\n       \"value\": \"$32 billion\",\n       \"kind\": \"actual\",\n       \"basis\": \"company inventory, as stated on the fiscal Q2 2027 call\",\n       \"party\": \"NVIDIA\"\n      }\n     ],\n     \"links\": [\n      {\n       \"question\": \"clean-4\",\n       \"new_question\": false,\n       \"reason\": \"Management's statement that inventory rose to $32 billion in preparation for the Vera Rubin launch is a sign the launch is being supplied ahead of shipments; it bears on the question of whether Vera Rubin reaches volume customer shipments and first revenue recognition by end-FY2027, but does not itself confirm shipments or revenue.\"\n      }\n     ]\n    },\n    {\n     \"id\": \"e5\",\n     \"summary\": \"NVIDIA management expects the newly launched Vera Rubin platform to make up about 20% of data center revenue in fiscal Q3 2027.\",\n     \"passages\": [\n      \"The newly launched Vera Rubin platform is one source of that revenue. Management expects it to make up about 20% of data center revenue in fiscal Q3 2027.\"\n     ],\n     \"statement_party\": \"NVIDIA\",\n     \"statement_date\": null,\n     \"figures\": [\n      {\n       \"n\": 1,\n       \"measure\": \"Vera Rubin share of data center revenue\",\n       \"period\": \"fiscal Q3 2027\",\n       \"value\": \"about 20%\",\n       \"kind\": \"guidance\",\n       \"basis\": \"share of data center revenue\",\n       \"party\": \"NVIDIA management\"\n      }\n     ],\n     \"links\": [\n      {\n       \"question\": \"clean-4\",\n       \"new_question\": false,\n       \"reason\": \"The company's own forecast that Vera Rubin will supply about 20% of data center revenue in fiscal Q3 2027 implies the platform is expected to generate revenue within FY2027; it bears on whether volume customer shipments and first revenue recognition occur by end-FY2027, though the forecast is not confirmation of shipments.\"\n      }\n     ]\n    },\n    {\n     \"id\": \"e7\",\n     \"summary\": \"NVIDIA guided gross margin for fiscal Q3 2027 to 74%, plus or minus half a point, down from 75% in fiscal Q2 2027, with management pointing to higher component costs, especially memory.\",\n     \"passages\": [\n      \"Gross margin, the share of revenue left after making the product, is a different measure from operating margin. For fiscal Q3 2027 it is guided to 74%, plus or minus half a point, down from 75% in fiscal Q2 2027. Management pointed to higher component costs, especially memory.\"\n     ],\n     \"statement_party\": \"NVIDIA\",\n     \"statement_date\": null,\n     \"figures\": [\n      {\n       \"n\": 1,\n       \"measure\": \"gross margin guidance\",\n       \"period\": \"fiscal Q3 2027\",\n       \"value\": \"74%\",\n       \"kind\": \"guidance\",\n       \"basis\": \"plus or minus half a point; company gross margin\",\n       \"party\": \"NVIDIA management\"\n      },\n      {\n       \"n\": 2,\n       \"measure\": \"gross margin\",\n       \"period\": \"fiscal Q2 2027\",\n       \"value\": \"75%\",\n       \"kind\": \"actual\",\n       \"basis\": \"company gross margin\",\n       \"party\": \"NVIDIA\"\n      }\n     ],\n     \"links\": \"none\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"id\": \"e8\",\n     \"summary\": \"Trefis reports NVIDIA's operating margin at 65%, its highest in ten years, against a three-year average of 59%.\",\n     \"passages\": [\n      \"NVIDIA’s operating margin, the share of revenue left after running costs, is 65%, its highest in ten years. Its three-year average is 59%.\"\n     ],\n     \"statement_party\": \"trefis.com\",\n     \"statement_date\": null,\n     \"figures\": [\n      {\n       \"n\": 1,\n       \"measure\": \"operating margin\",\n       \"period\": null,\n       \"value\": \"65%\",\n       \"kind\": \"actual\",\n       \"basis\": \"author's calculation from reported results; company operating margin\",\n       \"party\": \"trefis.com\"\n      },\n      {\n       \"n\": 2,\n       \"measure\": \"operating margin, three-year average\",\n       \"period\": \"three-year average\",\n       \"value\": \"59%\",\n       \"kind\": \"actual\",\n       \"basis\": \"author's calculation; three-year average, company operating margin\",\n       \"party\": \"trefis.com\"\n      }\n     ],\n     \"links\": \"none\"\n    }\n   ]\n  },\n  {\n   \"answer\": \"B46\",\n   \"article\": 5,\n   \"statements_with_figures\": [\n    {\n     \"id\": \"e1\",\n     \"summary\": \"NVIDIA reported $96.2 billion in revenue for its fiscal second quarter, 106% more than in the same period a year earlier, and produced a 75% gross margin, according to the Motley Fool's review of the results.\",\n     \"passages\": [\n      \"Nvidia ( NVDA +2.55% ) recently reported $96.2 billion in revenue for the fiscal second quarter, which was 106% more than in the same period last year. The company also produced a 75% gross margin\"\n     ],\n     \"statement_party\": \"NVIDIA\",\n     \"statement_date\": null,\n     \"figures\": [\n      {\n       \"n\": 1,\n       \"measure\": \"revenue\",\n       \"period\": \"fiscal second quarter\",\n       \"value\": \"$96.2 billion\",\n       \"kind\": \"actual\",\n       \"basis\": \"company-wide revenue, up 106% year over year\",\n       \"party\": \"NVIDIA\"\n      },\n      {\n       \"n\": 2,\n       \"measure\": \"gross margin\",\n       \"period\": \"fiscal second quarter\",\n       \"value\": \"75%\",\n       \"kind\": \"actual\",\n       \"basis\": \"company-wide gross margin\",\n       \"party\": \"NVIDIA\"\n      }\n     ],\n     \"links\": \"none\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"id\": \"e2\",\n     \"summary\": \"NVIDIA management told investors to expect roughly 70% revenue growth in the next fiscal year, the Motley Fool reported.\",\n     \"passages\": [\n      \"management told investors to expect roughly 70% revenue growth in the next fiscal year\"\n     ],\n     \"statement_party\": \"NVIDIA\",\n     \"statement_date\": null,\n     \"figures\": [\n      {\n       \"n\": 1,\n       \"measure\": \"revenue growth\",\n       \"period\": \"next fiscal year\",\n       \"value\": \"roughly 70%\",\n       \"kind\": \"guidance\",\n       \"basis\": \"management guidance as reported, company-wide\",\n       \"party\": \"NVIDIA\"\n      }\n     ],\n     \"links\": \"none\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"id\": \"e3\",\n     \"summary\": \"NVIDIA guided to $108 billion in revenue for the quarter following the reported fiscal second quarter, representing 17% sequential growth.\",\n     \"passages\": [\n      \"Nvidia guided to a jaw-dropping $108 billion in revenue for the current quarter, representing 17% sequential growth\"\n     ],\n     \"statement_party\": \"NVIDIA\",\n     \"statement_date\": null,\n     \"figures\": [\n      {\n       \"n\": 1,\n       \"measure\": \"revenue\",\n       \"period\": \"current quarter (fiscal third quarter)\",\n       \"value\": \"$108 billion\",\n       \"kind\": \"guidance\",\n       \"basis\": \"company guidance, representing 17% sequential growth\",\n       \"party\": \"NVIDIA\"\n      }\n     ],\n     \"links\": \"none\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"id\": \"e4\",\n     \"summary\": \"NVIDIA's total assets jumped more than 50% over the six months to the end of July, with receivables rising from $38.5 billion in January to $63.1 billion at the end of July and days sales outstanding rising from 45 to 60 days in one quarter, according to the Motley Fool's reading of the balance sheet.\",\n     \"passages\": [\n      \"Nvidia's total assets jumped by more than 50% over the past six months.\",\n      \"A big part of that is the company's receivables rising from $38.5 billion in January to $63.1 billion at the end of July.\",\n      \"Nvidia's DSO went from 45 to 60 days in one quarter.\"\n     ],\n     \"statement_party\": \"NVIDIA\",\n     \"statement_date\": null,\n     \"figures\": [\n      {\n       \"n\": 1,\n       \"measure\": \"receivables\",\n       \"period\": \"end of July (fiscal Q2)\",\n       \"value\": \"$63.1 billion\",\n       \"kind\": \"actual\",\n       \"basis\": \"up from $38.5 billion in January\",\n       \"party\": \"NVIDIA\"\n      },\n      {\n       \"n\": 2,\n       \"measure\": \"days sales outstanding\",\n       \"period\": \"one quarter (fiscal Q2)\",\n       \"value\": \"60 days\",\n       \"kind\": \"actual\",\n       \"basis\": \"up from 45 days in the prior quarter\",\n       \"party\": \"NVIDIA\"\n      },\n      {\n       \"n\": 3,\n       \"measure\": \"total assets\",\n       \"period\": \"past six months to end of July\",\n       \"value\": \"more than 50%\",\n       \"kind\": \"actual\",\n       \"basis\": \"growth over the six-month period\",\n       \"party\": \"NVIDIA\"\n      }\n     ],\n     \"links\": [\n      {\n       \"question\": \"clean-1\",\n       \"new_question\": false,\n       \"reason\": \"Receivables growth and the jump in days sales outstanding from 45 to 60 days bear on how much NVIDIA is financing customers' purchases, the same question as the thesis's guarantees and credit support, though these are unpaid invoices rather than disclosed guarantees.\"\n      }\n     ]\n    },\n    {\n     \"id\": \"e6\",\n     \"summary\": \"NVIDIA agreed to guarantee up to $105 billion of data center lease obligations for OpenAI, the Motley Fool reported as an example of 'circular' financial moves in which NVIDIA helps customers pay for its products.\",\n     \"passages\": [\n      \"For example, Nvidia agreed to guarantee up to $105 billion of data center lease obligations for OpenAI.\"\n     ],\n     \"statement_party\": \"NVIDIA\",\n     \"statement_date\": null,\n     \"figures\": [\n      {\n       \"n\": 1,\n       \"measure\": \"guarantee of data center lease obligations for OpenAI\",\n       \"period\": null,\n       \"value\": \"$105 billion\",\n       \"kind\": \"actual\",\n       \"basis\": \"up to; guarantee of OpenAI's data center lease obligations\",\n       \"party\": \"NVIDIA\"\n      }\n     ],\n     \"links\": [\n      {\n       \"question\": \"clean-1\",\n       \"new_question\": false,\n       \"reason\": \"A sized credit-support commitment of exactly the kind the thesis asks NVIDIA to disclose at its next annual report: a guarantee backing another party's lease obligations; the item restates it rather than reporting a new step.\"\n      }\n     ]\n    }\n   ]\n  },\n  {\n   \"answer\": \"B48\",\n   \"article\": 5,\n   \"statements_with_figures\": [\n    {\n     \"id\": \"e1\",\n     \"summary\": \"NVIDIA reported $96.2 billion in revenue for its fiscal second quarter, 106% more than in the same period a year earlier, at a 75% gross margin, according to Fool.com's account of the results.\",\n     \"passages\": [\n      \"Nvidia ( NVDA +2.55% ) recently reported $96.2 billion in revenue for the fiscal second quarter, which was 106% more than in the same period last year. The company also produced a 75% gross margin\"\n     ],\n     \"statement_party\": \"NVIDIA\",\n     \"statement_date\": null,\n     \"figures\": [\n      {\n       \"n\": 1,\n       \"measure\": \"revenue\",\n       \"period\": \"fiscal second quarter\",\n       \"value\": \"$96.2 billion\",\n       \"kind\": \"actual\",\n       \"basis\": null,\n       \"party\": \"NVIDIA\"\n      },\n      {\n       \"n\": 2,\n       \"measure\": \"revenue growth vs. same period last year\",\n       \"period\": \"fiscal second quarter\",\n       \"value\": \"106%\",\n       \"kind\": \"actual\",\n       \"basis\": null,\n       \"party\": \"NVIDIA\"\n      },\n      {\n       \"n\": 3,\n       \"measure\": \"gross margin\",\n       \"period\": \"fiscal second quarter\",\n       \"value\": \"75%\",\n       \"kind\": \"actual\",\n       \"basis\": null,\n       \"party\": \"NVIDIA\"\n      }\n     ],\n     \"links\": \"none\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"id\": \"e2\",\n     \"summary\": \"NVIDIA management told investors to expect roughly 70% revenue growth in the next fiscal year, Fool.com reported.\",\n     \"passages\": [\n      \"management told investors to expect roughly 70% revenue growth in the next fiscal year\"\n     ],\n     \"statement_party\": \"NVIDIA\",\n     \"statement_date\": null,\n     \"figures\": [\n      {\n       \"n\": 1,\n       \"measure\": \"revenue growth\",\n       \"period\": \"next fiscal year\",\n       \"value\": \"roughly 70%\",\n       \"kind\": \"guidance\",\n       \"basis\": null,\n       \"party\": \"NVIDIA\"\n      }\n     ],\n     \"links\": \"none\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"id\": \"e3\",\n     \"summary\": \"NVIDIA guided to $108 billion in revenue for the current quarter, representing 17% sequential growth.\",\n     \"passages\": [\n      \"Nvidia guided to a jaw-dropping $108 billion in revenue for the current quarter, representing 17% sequential growth.\"\n     ],\n     \"statement_party\": \"NVIDIA\",\n     \"statement_date\": null,\n     \"figures\": [\n      {\n       \"n\": 1,\n       \"measure\": \"revenue\",\n       \"period\": \"current quarter\",\n       \"value\": \"$108 billion\",\n       \"kind\": \"guidance\",\n       \"basis\": null,\n       \"party\": \"NVIDIA\"\n      },\n      {\n       \"n\": 2,\n       \"measure\": \"sequential revenue growth\",\n       \"period\": \"current quarter\",\n       \"value\": \"17%\",\n       \"kind\": \"guidance\",\n       \"basis\": null,\n       \"party\": \"NVIDIA\"\n      }\n     ],\n     \"links\": \"none\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"id\": \"e4\",\n     \"summary\": \"NVIDIA's total assets jumped by more than 50% over the past six months, with receivables rising from $38.5 billion in January to $63.1 billion at the end of July and days sales outstanding rising from 45 to 60 days in one quarter; NVIDIA said it extended payment terms on large orders from certain customers, which the article flags as a sign customers are taking longer to pay.\",\n     \"passages\": [\n      \"However, a closer look at the company's balance sheet shows that Nvidia's total assets jumped by more than 50% over the past six months.\",\n      \"A big part of that is the company's receivables rising from $38.5 billion in January to $63.1 billion at the end of July.\",\n      \"Nvidia's DSO went from 45 to 60 days in one quarter. Customers are taking longer to pay.\",\n      \"To be fair, Nvidia told us why. The company extended payment terms on large orders from certain customers.\"\n     ],\n     \"statement_party\": \"NVIDIA\",\n     \"statement_date\": null,\n     \"figures\": [\n      {\n       \"n\": 1,\n       \"measure\": \"total assets growth\",\n       \"period\": \"past six months\",\n       \"value\": \"more than 50%\",\n       \"kind\": \"actual\",\n       \"basis\": null,\n       \"party\": \"NVIDIA\"\n      },\n      {\n       \"n\": 2,\n       \"measure\": \"accounts receivable\",\n       \"period\": \"January\",\n       \"value\": \"$38.5 billion\",\n       \"kind\": \"actual\",\n       \"basis\": null,\n       \"party\": \"NVIDIA\"\n      },\n      {\n       \"n\": 3,\n       \"measure\": \"accounts receivable\",\n       \"period\": \"end of July\",\n       \"value\": \"$63.1 billion\",\n       \"kind\": \"actual\",\n       \"basis\": null,\n       \"party\": \"NVIDIA\"\n      },\n      {\n       \"n\": 4,\n       \"measure\": \"days sales outstanding (DSO)\",\n       \"period\": \"one quarter\",\n       \"value\": \"45 to 60 days\",\n       \"kind\": \"actual\",\n       \"basis\": null,\n       \"party\": \"NVIDIA\"\n      }\n     ],\n     \"links\": [\n      {\n       \"question\": \"clean-1\",\n       \"new_question\": false,\n       \"reason\": \"NVIDIA's extension of payment terms on large customer orders is a form of trade credit/customer financing, relevant to the thesis's question of how much credit support NVIDIA extends to the AI labs and cloud partners it sells to; the item discloses no amount of terms extended and this is trade credit, not a guarantee or revenue backstop.\"\n      }\n     ]\n    },\n    {\n     \"id\": \"e5\",\n     \"summary\": \"NVIDIA agreed to guarantee up to $105 billion of data center lease obligations for OpenAI, cited by Fool.com as part of a string of 'circular' financial moves in which NVIDIA helps customers pay for its products; the item gives no date for the agreement.\",\n     \"passages\": [\n      \"For example, Nvidia agreed to guarantee up to $105 billion of data center lease obligations for OpenAI.\"\n     ],\n     \"statement_party\": \"NVIDIA\",\n     \"statement_date\": null,\n     \"figures\": [\n      {\n       \"n\": 1,\n       \"measure\": \"guarantee of OpenAI data center lease obligations\",\n       \"period\": null,\n       \"value\": \"up to $105 billion\",\n       \"kind\": \"actual\",\n       \"basis\": \"up to\",\n       \"party\": \"NVIDIA\"\n      }\n     ],\n     \"links\": [\n      {\n       \"question\": \"clean-1\",\n       \"new_question\": false,\n       \"reason\": \"Sizes NVIDIA's guarantee of OpenAI's data center lease obligations at up to $105 billion — exactly the kind of credit-support commitment the thesis asks NVIDIA to disclose at its next annual report; the item does not say whether or how the guarantee has been reported in filings.\"\n      }\n     ]\n    },\n    {\n     \"id\": \"e6\",\n     \"summary\": \"NVIDIA has formed partnerships to raise over $500 billion of outside capital for AI infrastructure, according to Fool.com, which cites this alongside the OpenAI lease guarantee as evidence NVIDIA is helping customers pay for its products; the item gives no date or partner names.\",\n     \"passages\": [\n      \"It has formed partnerships to raise over $500 billion of outside capital for AI infrastructure.\"\n     ],\n     \"statement_party\": \"NVIDIA\",\n     \"statement_date\": null,\n     \"figures\": [\n      {\n       \"n\": 1,\n       \"measure\": \"outside capital raised for AI infrastructure through partnerships\",\n       \"period\": null,\n       \"value\": \"over $500 billion\",\n       \"kind\": \"actual\",\n       \"basis\": null,\n       \"party\": \"NVIDIA\"\n      }\n     ],\n     \"links\": [\n      {\n       \"question\": \"clean-1\",\n       \"new_question\": false,\n       \"reason\": \"Gives the scale (over $500 billion) of outside capital NVIDIA's partnerships raise for AI infrastructure; this is third-party capital rather than NVIDIA's own guarantee, but it bears on the thesis's comparison of guarantees/credit support versus equity and other support NVIDIA extends to AI labs and cloud partners.\"\n      }\n     ]\n    }\n   ]\n  },\n  {\n   \"answer\": \"B56\",\n   \"article\": 9,\n   \"statements_with_figures\": [\n    {\n     \"id\": \"ev-1\",\n     \"summary\": \"Zacks Consensus Estimates project Nvidia's upcoming-quarter EPS at $2.47, a 90.00% increase versus the same quarter a year earlier, and revenue at $109.02 billion, a 91.24% increase versus the year-ago quarter, according to the latest consensus cited by Zacks.\",\n     \"passages\": [\n      \"Analysts and investors alike will be keeping a close eye on the performance of Nvidia in its upcoming earnings disclosure. The company's upcoming EPS is projected at $2.47, signifying a 90.00% increase compared to the same quarter of the previous year. Simultaneously, our latest consensus estimate expects the revenue to be $109.02 billion, showing a 91.24% escalation compared to the year-ago quarter.\"\n     ],\n     \"statement_party\": \"Zacks Investment Research (Zacks Consensus Estimates)\",\n     \"statement_date\": \"2026-09-28\",\n     \"figures\": [\n      {\n       \"n\": 1,\n       \"measure\": \"EPS\",\n       \"period\": \"upcoming quarter (quarter not named)\",\n       \"value\": \"$2.47\",\n       \"kind\": \"estimate\",\n       \"basis\": \"Zacks Consensus Estimate; vs. same quarter of previous year (+90.00%)\",\n       \"party\": \"Zacks Investment Research (Zacks Consensus Estimates)\"\n      },\n      {\n       \"n\": 2,\n       \"measure\": \"Revenue\",\n       \"period\": \"upcoming quarter (quarter not named)\",\n       \"value\": \"$109.02 billion\",\n       \"kind\": \"estimate\",\n       \"basis\": \"Zacks Consensus Estimate; vs. year-ago quarter (+91.24%)\",\n       \"party\": \"Zacks Investment Research (Zacks Consensus Estimates)\"\n      }\n     ],\n     \"links\": \"none\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"id\": \"ev-2\",\n     \"summary\": \"Zacks Consensus Estimates project Nvidia's full-year earnings at $9.25 per share and revenue at $406.05 billion, which would represent increases of 93.92% and 88.04%, respectively, from the prior year.\",\n     \"passages\": [\n      \"For the full year, the Zacks Consensus Estimates are projecting earnings of $9.25 per share and revenue of $406.05 billion, which would represent changes of +93.92% and +88.04%, respectively, from the prior year.\"\n     ],\n     \"statement_party\": \"Zacks Investment Research (Zacks Consensus Estimates)\",\n     \"statement_date\": \"2026-09-28\",\n     \"figures\": [\n      {\n       \"n\": 1,\n       \"measure\": \"Earnings per share\",\n       \"period\": \"full year (fiscal year not specified)\",\n       \"value\": \"$9.25 per share\",\n       \"kind\": \"estimate\",\n       \"basis\": \"Zacks Consensus Estimate; +93.92% vs. prior year\",\n       \"party\": \"Zacks Investment Research (Zacks Consensus Estimates)\"\n      },\n      {\n       \"n\": 2,\n       \"measure\": \"Revenue\",\n       \"period\": \"full year (fiscal year not specified)\",\n       \"value\": \"$406.05 billion\",\n       \"kind\": \"estimate\",\n       \"basis\": \"Zacks Consensus Estimate; +88.04% vs. prior year\",\n       \"party\": \"Zacks Investment Research (Zacks Consensus Estimates)\"\n      }\n     ],\n     \"links\": \"none\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"id\": \"ev-3\",\n     \"summary\": \"The Zacks Consensus EPS estimate for Nvidia has been revised 4.73% upward over the past month, per Zacks.\",\n     \"passages\": [\n      \"Over the past month, the Zacks Consensus EPS estimate has shifted 4.73% upward.\"\n     ],\n     \"statement_party\": \"Zacks Investment Research (Zacks Consensus Estimates)\",\n     \"statement_date\": \"2026-09-28\",\n     \"figures\": [\n      {\n       \"n\": 1,\n       \"measure\": \"Zacks Consensus EPS estimate revision\",\n       \"period\": \"past month\",\n       \"value\": \"4.73%\",\n       \"kind\": \"estimate\",\n       \"basis\": \"Upward revision of consensus EPS over the past month\",\n       \"party\": \"Zacks Investment Research (Zacks Consensus Estimates)\"\n      }\n     ],\n     \"links\": \"none\"\n    }\n   ]\n  },\n  {\n   \"answer\": \"B58\",\n   \"article\": 9,\n   \"statements_with_figures\": [\n    {\n     \"id\": \"e1\",\n     \"summary\": \"The Zacks Consensus Estimates cited in the article project Nvidia's upcoming-quarter EPS at $2.47, a 90.00% increase versus the same quarter a year earlier, and upcoming-quarter revenue at $109.02 billion, a 91.24% increase versus the year-ago quarter.\",\n     \"passages\": [\n      \"Analysts and investors alike will be keeping a close eye on the performance of Nvidia in its upcoming earnings disclosure. The company's upcoming EPS is projected at $2.47, signifying a 90.00% increase compared to the same quarter of the previous year. Simultaneously, our latest consensus estimate expects the revenue to be $109.02 billion, showing a 91.24% escalation compared to the year-ago quarter.\"\n     ],\n     \"statement_party\": \"Zacks Consensus Estimates (Zacks Investment Research)\",\n     \"statement_date\": null,\n     \"figures\": [\n      {\n       \"n\": 1,\n       \"measure\": \"EPS (Zacks Consensus Estimate)\",\n       \"period\": \"upcoming quarter\",\n       \"value\": \"$2.47\",\n       \"kind\": \"estimate\",\n       \"basis\": \"consensus estimate; +90.00% versus the same quarter of the previous year\",\n       \"party\": \"Zacks Consensus Estimates (Zacks Investment Research)\"\n      },\n      {\n       \"n\": 2,\n       \"measure\": \"revenue (Zacks Consensus Estimate)\",\n       \"period\": \"upcoming quarter\",\n       \"value\": \"$109.02 billion\",\n       \"kind\": \"estimate\",\n       \"basis\": \"consensus estimate; +91.24% versus the year-ago quarter\",\n       \"party\": \"Zacks Consensus Estimates (Zacks Investment Research)\"\n      }\n     ],\n     \"links\": \"none\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"id\": \"e2\",\n     \"summary\": \"The Zacks Consensus Estimates cited in the article project Nvidia's full-year earnings at $9.25 per share, +93.92% versus the prior year, and full-year revenue at $406.05 billion, +88.04% versus the prior year.\",\n     \"passages\": [\n      \"For the full year, the Zacks Consensus Estimates are projecting earnings of $9.25 per share and revenue of $406.05 billion, which would represent changes of +93.92% and +88.04%, respectively, from the prior year.\"\n     ],\n     \"statement_party\": \"Zacks Consensus Estimates (Zacks Investment Research)\",\n     \"statement_date\": null,\n     \"figures\": [\n      {\n       \"n\": 1,\n       \"measure\": \"earnings per share (Zacks Consensus Estimate)\",\n       \"period\": \"full year\",\n       \"value\": \"$9.25 per share\",\n       \"kind\": \"estimate\",\n       \"basis\": \"consensus estimate; +93.92% versus the prior year\",\n       \"party\": \"Zacks Consensus Estimates (Zacks Investment Research)\"\n      },\n      {\n       \"n\": 2,\n       \"measure\": \"revenue (Zacks Consensus Estimate)\",\n       \"period\": \"full year\",\n       \"value\": \"$406.05 billion\",\n       \"kind\": \"estimate\",\n       \"basis\": \"consensus estimate; +88.04% versus the prior year\",\n       \"party\": \"Zacks Consensus Estimates (Zacks Investment Research)\"\n      }\n     ],\n     \"links\": \"none\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"id\": \"e3\",\n     \"summary\": \"Zacks reports that its Consensus EPS estimate for Nvidia has been revised 4.73% upward over the past month, a direction it reads as analysts turning more positive on the near-term business, and that Nvidia currently carries a Zacks Rank of #1 (Strong Buy).\",\n     \"passages\": [\n      \"Over the past month, the Zacks Consensus EPS estimate has shifted 4.73% upward. Right now, Nvidia possesses a Zacks Rank of #1 (Strong Buy).\"\n     ],\n     \"statement_party\": \"Zacks Investment Research\",\n     \"statement_date\": \"past month (relative date, as stated)\",\n     \"figures\": [\n      {\n       \"n\": 1,\n       \"measure\": \"Zacks Consensus EPS estimate revision\",\n       \"period\": \"past month\",\n       \"value\": \"4.73%\",\n       \"kind\": \"estimate\",\n       \"basis\": \"upward shift in the consensus EPS estimate over the past month\",\n       \"party\": \"Zacks Investment Research\"\n      }\n     ],\n     \"links\": \"none\"\n    }\n   ]\n  }\n ]\n}","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_1","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"python3 -c 'import json; p=json.load(open(\\\"docs/research/2026-11-14-use-acceptance/figure-fields-2/settle-packet-part2.json\\\")); [(print(\\\"ARTICLE\\\",a[\\\"article\\\"],a[\\\"title\\\"],a[\\\"publisher\\\"],a[\\\"publication_date\\\"],a[\\\"text\\\"])) for a in p[\\\"articles\\\"] if a[\\\"article\\\"] not in [2,5,7]]; print(\\\"ANSWERS\\\"); [(print(json.dumps({\\\"answer\\\":a[\\\"answer\\\"],\\\"article\\\":a[\\\"article\\\"],\\\"s\\\":[{\\\"id\\\":s[\\\"id\\\"],\\\"summary\\\":s[\\\"summary\\\"],\\\"party\\\":s[\\\"statement_party\\\"],\\\"date\\\":s[\\\"statement_date\\\"],\\\"figures\\\":s[\\\"figures\\\"],\\\"links\\\":s.get(\\\"links\\\",\\\"ABSENT\\\")} for s in a[\\\"statements_with_figures\\\"]]},ensure_ascii=False))) for a in p[\\\"answers\\\"]]'\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_1","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"python3 -c 'import json; p=json.load(open(\\\"docs/research/2026-11-14-use-acceptance/figure-fields-2/settle-packet-part2.json\\\")); [(print(\\\"ARTICLE\\\",a[\\\"article\\\"],a[\\\"title\\\"],a[\\\"publisher\\\"],a[\\\"publication_date\\\"],a[\\\"text\\\"])) for a in p[\\\"articles\\\"] if a[\\\"article\\\"] not in [2,5,7]]; print(\\\"ANSWERS\\\"); [(print(json.dumps({\\\"answer\\\":a[\\\"answer\\\"],\\\"article\\\":a[\\\"article\\\"],\\\"s\\\":[{\\\"id\\\":s[\\\"id\\\"],\\\"summary\\\":s[\\\"summary\\\"],\\\"party\\\":s[\\\"statement_party\\\"],\\\"date\\\":s[\\\"statement_date\\\"],\\\"figures\\\":s[\\\"figures\\\"],\\\"links\\\":s.get(\\\"links\\\",\\\"ABSENT\\\")} for s in a[\\\"statements_with_figures\\\"]]},ensure_ascii=False))) for a in p[\\\"answers\\\"]]'\"","aggregated_output":"ARTICLE 8 Credo vs. Nvidia: Which AI Chip Stock Is a Better Buy in 2026? fool.com 2026-09-25 The artificial intelligence boom has transformed the semiconductor landscape. Deciding between a high-growth connectivity specialist like Credo Technology Group ( CRDO +5.75% ) and the industry giant Nvidia ( NVDA +2.55% ) requires a look at their specific niches.\nBoth companies are central to modern data centers, but they solve different problems. Credo provides the high-speed wiring and connectivity chips that keep data moving, while Nvidia supplies the massive processing power AI models require. Comparing these two reveals how different players profit from the same infrastructure trend.\nCredo designs high-speed copper and optical interconnect solutions essential for modern AI-driven infrastructure. The company operates as a niche provider of high-speed connectivity solutions among semiconductor stocks serving AI data centers. Credo relies on a limited number of customers for a substantial portion of its revenue, with sales to its top 10 customers accounting for approximately 90% of total revenue in fiscal 2026. This level of customer concentration adds risk to the business. The company also maintains a partnership with Oracle ( ORCL -0.13% ) to develop ZeroFlap optics to improve reliability in AI data centers.\nFinancial performance has accelerated significantly recently. In fiscal 2026, which ended in May 2026, revenue reached nearly $1.3 billion, up roughly 205.7% from the previous year. This growth helped the company achieve a net income of approximately $472.3 million. The net margin for the period was approximately 35.4%, a substantial improvement from the 11.9% net margin reported in the prior fiscal year.\nThe balance sheet remains highly liquid. As of its May 2026 balance sheet, the current ratio (a measure of short-term debt-paying ability) is nearly 10.2x. The debt-to-equity ratio (total debt compared to equity) is 0.0x, indicating no debt relative to equity. Free cash flow (cash from operations minus capital expenditures) was nearly $407.0 million. Stock-based compensation represented roughly 39.3% of operating cash flow, which inflates reported cash generation because SBC is a non-cash expense added back in the cash flow statement.\nNvidia is the dominant provider of GPU-accelerated computing platforms used in gaming, professional visualization, and data centers. The company sells its products to massive cloud service providers and AI model makers globally. Nvidia relies on a concentrated customer base, with sales to two direct customers representing 22% and 14% of total revenue in fiscal 2026, respectively. This level of customer concentration adds a layer of risk to the business. The company also recently agreed to acquire Hugging Face for approximately $12.9 billion to expand its software capabilities.\nRevenue growth remains a primary driver for the business. In fiscal 2026, which ended in January 2026, revenue reached nearly $215.9 billion, up roughly 65.5% year over year. This scale produced net income of approximately $120.1 billion. The net margin remained remarkably high at approximately 55.6%, consistent with the 55.8% net margin achieved in the previous fiscal year.\nStrong profitability supports a robust capital structure. As of its January 2026 balance sheet, the current ratio is approximately 3.9x. The debt-to-equity ratio (which compares total debt to shareholder equity) is under 0.1x, meaning the company carries very little debt. Free cash flow (cash from operations minus capital expenditures) reached nearly $96.7 billion for the fiscal year. This massive cash generation provides significant flexibility for research, development, and strategic acquisitions.\nCredo faces intense competition from larger, well-established semiconductor companies and potential new entrants in the data infrastructure market. The company depends heavily on a small number of customers, making it vulnerable to losses or shifts in purchasing patterns. Its business model relies on third-party manufacturing partners, exposing the company to supply chain disruptions and geopolitical risks regarding operations in Taiwan and mainland China. Furthermore, evolving international trade controls and export restrictions could restrict its ability to serve key markets.\nNvidia faces significant risks from global geopolitical tensions and export control regulations, particularly for high-performance AI chips. These restrictions have impacted sales to China and could continue to limit market access. The company faces intense competition from other hardware suppliers and internal development efforts by customers like Microsoft ( MSFT +1.22% ) , Amazon ( AMZN +0.51% ) , and Alphabet ( GOOGL -0.61% ) ( GOOG -0.77% ) . Nvidia also manages various legal challenges, including inquiries from competition regulators worldwide regarding its market practices.\nNvidia trades at a lower forward P/E based on future earnings estimates, while both carry a high P/S ratio compared to historical averages.\nI'd buy Nvidia, and it isn't close. Here's why:\nCredo's growth is not the issue. Revenue in its latest quarter more than doubled from a year earlier, and management guided the current quarter higher still. The stock fell anyway because gross margin guidance came in flat, and shares now sit well below their 52-week high. That's the problem with a company that gets about 90% of its revenue from 10 customers. Any hint of slower spending from one of them moves the stock, and the valuation leaves no room for a miss.\nNvidia is growing nearly as fast at a scale that makes Credo look like a rounding error. Revenue in its latest quarter also more than doubled, with almost all of it coming from data center customers, and management's outlook for the current quarter assumes no data center compute sales to China at all. Nvidia has its own concentration risk, with two customers making up more than a third of sales, and the Hugging Face deal shows it's willing to spend to stay ahead.\nFor a long-term investor, Nvidia offers the same AI infrastructure exposure at a lower price per dollar of expected earnings, with far less dependence on any single buyer. Credo belongs in a portfolio only as a small position you can afford to watch swing.\nCredo more than tripled revenue last year but leans on 10 customers for 90% of sales, while Nvidia is doubling revenue at a scale nobody else in AI hardware can match.\nBy Mike Schwenk – Updated Sep 25, 2026 at 3:00PM EST\nCredo makes the high-speed connectivity chips and cables that move data between servers inside AI data centers, and revenue more than tripled in fiscal 2026.\nNvidia's data center business brought in $89 billion in its latest quarter alone, and management guided the next quarter to $108 billion, excluding any China compute sales.\nBoth companies depend on a small group of customers, but Nvidia's scale, margins, and lower valuation make it the stronger buy today.\nARTICLE 9 Why the Market Dipped But Nvidia (NVDA) Gained Today zacks.com 2026-09-28 In the latest trading session, Nvidia (NVDA) closed at $228.86, marking a +1.68% move from the previous day. The stock's change was more than the S&P 500's daily loss of 0.77%. Meanwhile, the Dow experienced a drop of 0.67%, and the technology-dominated Nasdaq saw a decrease of 0.92%.\nShares of the maker of graphics chips for gaming and artificial intelligence have appreciated by 3.46% over the course of the past month, underperforming the Computer and Technology sector's gain of 6.26%, and outperforming the S&P 500's gain of 0.96%.\nAnalysts and investors alike will be keeping a close eye on the performance of Nvidia in its upcoming earnings disclosure. The company's upcoming EPS is projected at $2.47, signifying a 90.00% increase compared to the same quarter of the previous year. Simultaneously, our latest consensus estimate expects the revenue to be $109.02 billion, showing a 91.24% escalation compared to the year-ago quarter.\nFor the full year, the Zacks Consensus Estimates are projecting earnings of $9.25 per share and revenue of $406.05 billion, which would represent changes of +93.92% and +88.04%, respectively, from the prior year.\nInvestors should also take note of any recent adjustments to analyst estimates for Nvidia. Recent revisions tend to reflect the latest near-term business trends. Consequently, upward revisions in estimates express analysts' positivity towards the business operations and its ability to generate profits.\nBased on our research, we believe these estimate revisions are directly related to near-term stock moves. We developed the Zacks Rank to capitalize on this phenomenon. Our system takes these estimate changes into account and delivers a clear, actionable rating model.\nThe Zacks Rank system, which varies between #1 (Strong Buy) and #5 (Strong Sell), carries an impressive track record of exceeding expectations, confirmed by external audits, with stocks at #1 delivering an average annual return of +25% since 1988. Over the past month, the Zacks Consensus EPS estimate has shifted 4.73% upward. Right now, Nvidia possesses a Zacks Rank of #1 (Strong Buy).\nValuation is also important, so investors should note that Nvidia has a Forward P/E ratio of 24.34 right now. This represents a discount compared to its industry average Forward P/E of 38.49.\nIt is also worth noting that NVDA currently has a PEG ratio of 1.74. The PEG ratio bears resemblance to the frequently used P/E ratio, but this parameter also includes the company's expected earnings growth trajectory. The average PEG ratio for the Semiconductor - General industry stood at 1.74 at the close of the market yesterday.\nThe Semiconductor - General industry is part of the Computer and Technology sector. At present, this industry carries a Zacks Industry Rank of 49, placing it within the top 20% of over 250 industries.\nThe Zacks Industry Rank gauges the strength of our industry groups by measuring the average Zacks Rank of the individual stocks within the groups. Our research shows that the top 50% rated industries outperform the bottom half by a factor of 2 to 1.\nBe sure to use Zacks.com to monitor all these stock-influencing metrics, and more, throughout the forthcoming trading sessions.\nARTICLE 10 When Should You Buy NVIDIA Stock After This Run? trefis.com 2026-09-28 When Should You Buy NVIDIA Stock After This Run?\nA $10,000 holding in NVIDIA (NVDA) stock three months ago was worth about $11,700 as of September 25, 2026. Yet against profits, the shares still trade near the low end of their ten-year range. If you have been waiting to buy NVIDIA, have you already missed it?\nHave You Missed The Low Multiple On NVIDIA Stock?\nNot if you judge it by earnings against its own past. The price-to-earnings ratio, or P/E, is the share price divided by a year of profit per share. NVIDIA trades at 28.1 times earnings , above the 22.1 for the S&P 500. Over the past ten years its P/E has ranged from 19.6 to 143.1, and today it is near the bottom.\nBut a low P/E only stays low if profit holds up. NVIDIA’s operating margin, the share of revenue left after running costs, is 65%, its highest in ten years. Its three-year average is 59%. If margins slipped back toward that average, the same price would buy less profit.\nCash looks less comfortable than earnings. Against operating cash flow, the cash the business brings in from running itself, the stock costs 40.4 times. The S&P 500 costs 14.5 times. Part of the cash has gone into inventory. On the fiscal Q2 2027 call, management said inventory rose to $32 billion as NVIDIA prepared for the Vera Rubin launch. On earnings or on cash, a price built on peak margins needs profit growth to continue.\nHow Much Revenue Is NVIDIA Stock Counting On?\nThe price appears to assume more revenue than NVIDIA itself has forecast for fiscal Q3 2027. Management guided revenue for that quarter to $108 billion, plus or minus 2%. The consensus forecast for that quarter is $111.3 billion, above the top of that range.\nThat expectation has a record behind it. Management said fiscal Q2 2027 revenue of $96 billion more than doubled from a year earlier. Growth also sped up for the fourth quarter in a row.\nThe newly launched Vera Rubin platform is one source of that revenue. Management expects it to make up about 20% of data center revenue in fiscal Q3 2027. Management says supply, not demand, is the limit. Management expects supply to remain a bottleneck at least through the end of fiscal 2028.\nGross margin, the share of revenue left after making the product, is a different measure from operating margin. For fiscal Q3 2027 it is guided to 74%, plus or minus half a point, down from 75% in fiscal Q2 2027. Management pointed to higher component costs, especially memory. The next report has to beat NVIDIA’s own revenue forecast. The stock has not always taken its reports well.\nNVIDIA’s Fiscal Q3 2027 Results Land in November\nNVIDIA is expected to report on or around November 17, 2026. That report covers fiscal Q3 2027, and it will show whether revenue matches what the price appears to assume.\nThe stock’s record after reports runs both ways. Over the last six reports, NVIDIA shares fell after four and rose after two, measured over two trading days. The biggest drop was 9.4%, after the February 25, 2026, report. The biggest gain was 3.8%, after the August 26, 2026, report.\nA broad market sell-off is a separate risk. During the 2025 tariff shock, NVIDIA stock fell 32% from peak to low, against 19% for the S&P 500.\nIf fiscal Q3 2027 revenue lands below $111.3 billion, the price will have asked for more than NVIDIA delivered. Buying now would then prove early. Suppose revenue clears $111.3 billion and gross margin comes in at 74% or better. The business will then have kept pace with the run. Buying at today’s price would not have been too late.\nANSWERS\n{\"answer\": \"B24\", \"article\": 2, \"s\": [{\"id\": \"e1\", \"summary\": \"Nvidia's revenue and net income are each expected to grow by roughly 90% or more in its current fiscal year, according to estimates reported by Bloomberg, even as the stock trades near its lowest valuation in more than a decade.\", \"party\": \"Bloomberg (compiled estimates)\", \"date\": null, \"figures\": [{\"n\": 1, \"measure\": \"Revenue growth\", \"period\": \"Current fiscal year\", \"value\": \"roughly 90% or more\", \"kind\": \"estimate\", \"basis\": \"Company-level revenue growth expected, per Bloomberg\", \"party\": \"Bloomberg (compiled estimates)\"}, {\"n\": 2, \"measure\": \"Net income growth\", \"period\": \"Current fiscal year\", \"value\": \"roughly 90% or more\", \"kind\": \"estimate\", \"basis\": \"Company-level net income growth expected, per Bloomberg\", \"party\": \"Bloomberg (compiled estimates)\"}], \"links\": \"none\"}, {\"id\": \"e2\", \"summary\": \"Nvidia's gross margin came in at 75% in the last reported quarter, and analyst estimates compiled by Bloomberg show it sliding under 72% by the fourth quarter, with a recovery expected thereafter; rising costs for key components such as memory chips are cited as a central factor.\", \"party\": \"Nvidia (reported gross margin); Bloomberg-compiled analyst estimates\", \"date\": null, \"figures\": [{\"n\": 1, \"measure\": \"Gross margin\", \"period\": \"Last quarter\", \"value\": \"75%\", \"kind\": \"actual\", \"basis\": \"Company-level gross margin, as reported\", \"party\": \"Nvidia (reported gross margin); Bloomberg-compiled analyst estimates\"}, {\"n\": 2, \"measure\": \"Gross margin\", \"period\": \"Fourth quarter\", \"value\": \"under 72%\", \"kind\": \"estimate\", \"basis\": \"Analyst estimates compiled by Bloomberg\", \"party\": \"Nvidia (reported gross margin); Bloomberg-compiled analyst estimates\"}], \"links\": \"none\"}, {\"id\": \"e3\", \"summary\": \"Nvidia reported second-quarter revenue that more than doubled year over year and projected 70% revenue growth for fiscal 2028, well above the 45% analysts had expected at the time; the stock rose roughly 7% after the earnings report.\", \"party\": \"Nvidia (results and guidance); analysts (prior expectation)\", \"date\": null, \"figures\": [{\"n\": 1, \"measure\": \"Revenue growth\", \"period\": \"Second quarter\", \"value\": \"more than doubled\", \"kind\": \"actual\", \"basis\": \"Year-over-year company revenue growth, as reported\", \"party\": \"Nvidia (results and guidance); analysts (prior expectation)\"}, {\"n\": 2, \"measure\": \"Revenue growth guidance\", \"period\": \"Fiscal 2028\", \"value\": \"70%\", \"kind\": \"guidance\", \"basis\": \"Company projection, per the article\", \"party\": \"Nvidia (results and guidance); analysts (prior expectation)\"}, {\"n\": 3, \"measure\": \"Revenue growth expectation\", \"period\": \"Fiscal 2028\", \"value\": \"45%\", \"kind\": \"estimate\", \"basis\": \"What analysts had expected at the time, per the article\", \"party\": \"Nvidia (results and guidance); analysts (prior expectation)\"}], \"links\": \"none\"}]}\n{\"answer\": \"B27\", \"article\": 7, \"s\": [{\"id\": \"ev-1\", \"summary\": \"NVIDIA management expects Vera Rubin, its newest platform, to be the fastest product ramp in the company's history, and sizes NVIDIA's revenue opportunity at about $40 billion per gigawatt of capacity with Vera Rubin, against $25 billion with Blackwell.\", \"party\": \"NVIDIA management\", \"date\": null, \"figures\": [{\"n\": 1, \"measure\": \"revenue opportunity per gigawatt of capacity with Vera Rubin\", \"period\": null, \"value\": \"about $40 billion per gigawatt\", \"kind\": \"estimate\", \"basis\": \"management's sizing, compared with $25 billion per gigawatt with Blackwell\", \"party\": \"NVIDIA management\"}], \"links\": [{\"question\": \"clean-4\", \"new_question\": false, \"reason\": \"Management's expectation that Vera Rubin is the fastest ramp in company history and its revenue intensity per gigawatt ($40 billion vs $25 billion for Blackwell) bears on the scale and timing of the Vera Rubin ramp the thesis tracks, though the item gives no shipment date or confirmation of volume customer shipments or first revenue recognition.\"}]}, {\"id\": \"ev-3\", \"summary\": \"NVIDIA management guided to about 70% revenue growth for fiscal 2028 — the first time it has guided a full year ahead — with the CEO saying demand is much greater than 70% and that supply is what holds the guide there.\", \"party\": \"NVIDIA management (CEO)\", \"date\": null, \"figures\": [{\"n\": 1, \"measure\": \"revenue growth\", \"period\": \"fiscal 2028\", \"value\": \"about 70%\", \"kind\": \"guidance\", \"basis\": \"company's first full-year-ahead guide; management says supply, not demand, holds the guide there\", \"party\": \"NVIDIA management (CEO)\"}], \"links\": \"none\"}, {\"id\": \"ev-5\", \"summary\": \"NVIDIA has invested nearly $50 billion in frontier AI labs, part of a demand picture the article says leans on NVIDIA's own balance sheet; management concedes some will call this circular financing and says the compute can be redeployed to other customers.\", \"party\": \"NVIDIA\", \"date\": null, \"figures\": [{\"n\": 1, \"measure\": \"investment in frontier AI labs\", \"period\": null, \"value\": \"nearly $50 billion\", \"kind\": \"actual\", \"basis\": \"cumulative amount invested by NVIDIA, as reported by the article\", \"party\": \"NVIDIA\"}], \"links\": [{\"question\": \"clean-1\", \"new_question\": false, \"reason\": \"Reports the equity-investment side of the comparison the thesis tracks — nearly $50 billion invested in frontier AI labs — and management's response to 'circular financing' criticism; the item gives no guarantee, credit-support or revenue-backstop amount.\"}]}, {\"id\": \"ev-6\", \"summary\": \"NVIDIA guided its gross margin lower, to settle in the 72% to 73% range in fiscal 2028, as memory prices rose more than management expected, partly offset by price increases NVIDIA has already executed.\", \"party\": \"NVIDIA management\", \"date\": null, \"figures\": [{\"n\": 1, \"measure\": \"gross margin\", \"period\": \"fiscal 2028\", \"value\": \"72% to 73%\", \"kind\": \"guidance\", \"basis\": \"guided range; memory prices higher than management expected, helped by price increases already executed (GAAP/non-GAAP basis not stated)\", \"party\": \"NVIDIA management\"}], \"links\": \"none\"}, {\"id\": \"ev-7\", \"summary\": \"The 33 analyst estimates of NVIDIA's fiscal 2028 earnings span $12.62 to $17.49 a share, a wide band for the year on which its forward multiple is priced.\", \"party\": \"33 unnamed analyst estimates (as reported by Trefis)\", \"date\": null, \"figures\": [{\"n\": 1, \"measure\": \"EPS estimates (range across 33 analysts)\", \"period\": \"fiscal 2028\", \"value\": \"$12.62 to $17.49 a share\", \"kind\": \"estimate\", \"basis\": \"range of 33 analyst estimates\", \"party\": \"33 unnamed analyst estimates (as reported by Trefis)\"}], \"links\": \"none\"}]}\n{\"answer\": \"B29\", \"article\": 2, \"s\": [{\"id\": \"e1\", \"summary\": \"Quartz, citing Bloomberg-compiled expectations, reports that NVIDIA's revenue and net income are each expected to grow by roughly 90% or more in its current fiscal year, even as the stock trades below 17 times forward earnings.\", \"party\": \"Bloomberg (compiled analyst estimates)\", \"date\": null, \"figures\": [{\"n\": 1, \"measure\": \"revenue growth\", \"period\": \"current fiscal year (FY2027)\", \"value\": \"roughly 90% or more\", \"kind\": \"estimate\", \"basis\": \"consensus expectation compiled by Bloomberg, per Quartz\", \"party\": \"Bloomberg (compiled analyst estimates)\"}, {\"n\": 2, \"measure\": \"net income growth\", \"period\": \"current fiscal year (FY2027)\", \"value\": \"roughly 90% or more\", \"kind\": \"estimate\", \"basis\": \"consensus expectation compiled by Bloomberg, per Quartz\", \"party\": \"Bloomberg (compiled analyst estimates)\"}], \"links\": \"none\"}, {\"id\": \"e2\", \"summary\": \"At NVIDIA's fiscal second-quarter earnings (reported before 22 September 2026), the company's revenue more than doubled year over year and NVIDIA projected 70% revenue growth for fiscal 2028, well above the 45% analysts had expected at the time.\", \"party\": \"NVIDIA\", \"date\": null, \"figures\": [{\"n\": 1, \"measure\": \"revenue growth\", \"period\": \"fiscal second quarter (FY2027)\", \"value\": \"more than doubled\", \"kind\": \"actual\", \"basis\": \"year-over-year, as reported by Quartz\", \"party\": \"NVIDIA\"}, {\"n\": 2, \"measure\": \"revenue growth guidance\", \"period\": \"fiscal 2028\", \"value\": \"70%\", \"kind\": \"guidance\", \"basis\": \"company projection made at the fiscal Q2 earnings\", \"party\": \"NVIDIA\"}, {\"n\": 3, \"measure\": \"analyst revenue growth expectation\", \"period\": \"fiscal 2028\", \"value\": \"45%\", \"kind\": \"estimate\", \"basis\": \"analysts' expectation at the time of the Q2 earnings, per Quartz\", \"party\": \"NVIDIA\"}], \"links\": \"none\"}, {\"id\": \"e3\", \"summary\": \"NVIDIA's gross margin came in at 75% in its last reported quarter (fiscal Q2, before 22 September 2026), according to Quartz.\", \"party\": \"NVIDIA\", \"date\": null, \"figures\": [{\"n\": 1, \"measure\": \"gross margin\", \"period\": \"last quarter (fiscal Q2 FY2027)\", \"value\": \"75%\", \"kind\": \"actual\", \"basis\": null, \"party\": \"NVIDIA\"}], \"links\": \"none\"}, {\"id\": \"e4\", \"summary\": \"Analyst estimates compiled by Bloomberg, reported by Quartz, show NVIDIA's gross margin sliding from 75% to under 72% by the fourth quarter, with a recovery expected thereafter; rising costs for key components such as memory chips are described as a central factor.\", \"party\": \"Bloomberg (compiled analyst estimates)\", \"date\": null, \"figures\": [{\"n\": 1, \"measure\": \"gross margin\", \"period\": \"fourth quarter (FY2027)\", \"value\": \"under 72%\", \"kind\": \"estimate\", \"basis\": \"analyst estimates compiled by Bloomberg\", \"party\": \"Bloomberg (compiled analyst estimates)\"}], \"links\": [{\"question\": \"clean-2\", \"new_question\": false, \"reason\": \"Rising costs for key components such as memory chips are the stated driver of the expected margin squeeze; this bears on the input-cost side of the purchase and supply commitments whose disclosed dollar amount clean-2 tracks. It is an input-cost signal only: the item gives no commitment, volume or purchase-obligation figures.\"}]}]}\n{\"answer\": \"B33\", \"article\": 8, \"s\": [{\"id\": \"e1\", \"summary\": \"NVIDIA's fiscal 2026 (year ended January 2026) revenue reached nearly $215.9 billion, up roughly 65.5% year over year, producing net income of approximately $120.1 billion and a net margin of approximately 55.6%, consistent with the 55.8% net margin of the prior fiscal year, according to Fool.com's comparison article.\", \"party\": \"NVIDIA\", \"date\": null, \"figures\": [{\"n\": 1, \"measure\": \"revenue\", \"period\": \"fiscal 2026 (ended January 2026)\", \"value\": \"nearly $215.9 billion\", \"kind\": \"actual\", \"basis\": \"company total revenue, fiscal year\", \"party\": \"NVIDIA\"}, {\"n\": 2, \"measure\": \"revenue growth year over year\", \"period\": \"fiscal 2026\", \"value\": \"roughly 65.5%\", \"kind\": \"actual\", \"basis\": \"company total revenue, fiscal year\", \"party\": \"NVIDIA\"}, {\"n\": 3, \"measure\": \"net income\", \"period\": \"fiscal 2026\", \"value\": \"approximately $120.1 billion\", \"kind\": \"actual\", \"basis\": \"company total, fiscal year\", \"party\": \"NVIDIA\"}, {\"n\": 4, \"measure\": \"net margin\", \"period\": \"fiscal 2026\", \"value\": \"approximately 55.6%\", \"kind\": \"actual\", \"basis\": \"company total; prior fiscal year 55.8%\", \"party\": \"NVIDIA\"}], \"links\": \"ABSENT\"}, {\"id\": \"e2\", \"summary\": \"NVIDIA's total revenue more than doubled year over year in its latest reported quarter, with almost all of it coming from data center customers, and its data center business generated $89 billion in that quarter alone, per Fool.com.\", \"party\": \"NVIDIA\", \"date\": null, \"figures\": [{\"n\": 1, \"measure\": \"data center revenue\", \"period\": \"latest reported quarter (as of 25 September 2026 article)\", \"value\": \"$89 billion\", \"kind\": \"actual\", \"basis\": \"data center business\", \"party\": \"NVIDIA\"}], \"links\": \"ABSENT\"}, {\"id\": \"e3\", \"summary\": \"NVIDIA management guided its next quarter to $108 billion of revenue, with an outlook that assumes no data center compute sales to China at all (excluding any China compute sales), as reported by Fool.com.\", \"party\": \"NVIDIA\", \"date\": null, \"figures\": [{\"n\": 1, \"measure\": \"revenue guidance\", \"period\": \"next quarter (quarter following the latest reported quarter)\", \"value\": \"$108 billion\", \"kind\": \"guidance\", \"basis\": \"company outlook, excluding any China compute sales\", \"party\": \"NVIDIA\"}], \"links\": \"ABSENT\"}, {\"id\": \"e4\", \"summary\": \"NVIDIA recently agreed to acquire Hugging Face for approximately $12.9 billion to expand its software capabilities, as reported by Fool.com; the item does not say the transaction has closed.\", \"party\": \"NVIDIA\", \"date\": \"recently\", \"figures\": [{\"n\": 1, \"measure\": \"acquisition consideration for Hugging Face\", \"period\": null, \"value\": \"approximately $12.9 billion\", \"kind\": \"actual\", \"basis\": \"agreed acquisition, not stated as completed\", \"party\": \"NVIDIA\"}], \"links\": \"ABSENT\"}, {\"id\": \"e5\", \"summary\": \"NVIDIA disclosed that sales to two direct customers represented 22% and 14% of total revenue in fiscal 2026, respectively, a customer concentration the article flags as a risk.\", \"party\": \"NVIDIA\", \"date\": null, \"figures\": [{\"n\": 1, \"measure\": \"sales to largest direct customer as share of total revenue\", \"period\": \"fiscal 2026\", \"value\": \"22%\", \"kind\": \"actual\", \"basis\": \"direct customer, company total revenue\", \"party\": \"NVIDIA\"}, {\"n\": 2, \"measure\": \"sales to second direct customer as share of total revenue\", \"period\": \"fiscal 2026\", \"value\": \"14%\", \"kind\": \"actual\", \"basis\": \"direct customer, company total revenue\", \"party\": \"NVIDIA\"}], \"links\": \"ABSENT\"}]}\n{\"answer\": \"B40\", \"article\": 10, \"s\": [{\"id\": \"ev-1\", \"summary\": \"On NVIDIA's fiscal Q2 2027 earnings call, management said inventory rose to $32 billion as the company prepared for the Vera Rubin launch.\", \"party\": \"NVIDIA\", \"date\": null, \"figures\": [{\"n\": 1, \"measure\": \"inventory\", \"period\": \"fiscal Q2 2027\", \"value\": \"$32 billion\", \"kind\": \"actual\", \"basis\": null, \"party\": \"NVIDIA\"}], \"links\": [{\"question\": \"clean-4\", \"new_question\": false, \"reason\": \"A $32 billion inventory build made in preparation for the Vera Rubin launch is a supply-side signal on how far the ramp toward volume shipments of Vera Rubin has progressed; it does not itself confirm shipments or revenue recognition.\"}]}, {\"id\": \"ev-2\", \"summary\": \"NVIDIA management guided fiscal Q3 2027 revenue to $108 billion, plus or minus 2%, while the consensus forecast for that quarter is $111.3 billion, above the top of that guidance range.\", \"party\": \"NVIDIA (guidance); consensus estimate as reported by Trefis\", \"date\": null, \"figures\": [{\"n\": 1, \"measure\": \"revenue guidance\", \"period\": \"fiscal Q3 2027\", \"value\": \"$108 billion\", \"kind\": \"guidance\", \"basis\": \"plus or minus 2%\", \"party\": \"NVIDIA (guidance); consensus estimate as reported by Trefis\"}, {\"n\": 2, \"measure\": \"consensus revenue forecast\", \"period\": \"fiscal Q3 2027\", \"value\": \"$111.3 billion\", \"kind\": \"estimate\", \"basis\": \"consensus forecast, above the top of NVIDIA's guidance range\", \"party\": \"NVIDIA (guidance); consensus estimate as reported by Trefis\"}], \"links\": \"none\"}, {\"id\": \"ev-3\", \"summary\": \"NVIDIA management said fiscal Q2 2027 revenue was $96 billion, more than double the year-earlier figure, with growth accelerating for the fourth consecutive quarter.\", \"party\": \"NVIDIA\", \"date\": null, \"figures\": [{\"n\": 1, \"measure\": \"revenue\", \"period\": \"fiscal Q2 2027\", \"value\": \"$96 billion\", \"kind\": \"actual\", \"basis\": \"more than doubled from a year earlier; growth accelerated for the fourth consecutive quarter\", \"party\": \"NVIDIA\"}], \"links\": \"none\"}, {\"id\": \"ev-4\", \"summary\": \"NVIDIA guided gross margin for fiscal Q3 2027 to 74%, plus or minus half a point, down from 75% in fiscal Q2 2027, with management pointing to higher component costs, especially memory.\", \"party\": \"NVIDIA\", \"date\": null, \"figures\": [{\"n\": 1, \"measure\": \"gross margin guidance\", \"period\": \"fiscal Q3 2027\", \"value\": \"74%\", \"kind\": \"guidance\", \"basis\": \"plus or minus half a point; attributed to higher component costs, especially memory\", \"party\": \"NVIDIA\"}, {\"n\": 2, \"measure\": \"gross margin\", \"period\": \"fiscal Q2 2027\", \"value\": \"75%\", \"kind\": \"actual\", \"basis\": null, \"party\": \"NVIDIA\"}], \"links\": \"none\"}, {\"id\": \"ev-5\", \"summary\": \"NVIDIA management expects the newly launched Vera Rubin platform to make up about 20% of its data center revenue in fiscal Q3 2027.\", \"party\": \"NVIDIA\", \"date\": null, \"figures\": [{\"n\": 1, \"measure\": \"Vera Rubin share of data center revenue\", \"period\": \"fiscal Q3 2027\", \"value\": \"about 20%\", \"kind\": \"guidance\", \"basis\": \"of data center revenue; management expectation\", \"party\": \"NVIDIA\"}], \"links\": [{\"question\": \"clean-4\", \"new_question\": false, \"reason\": \"Management's expectation that Vera Rubin will already contribute about 20% of data center revenue in fiscal Q3 2027 bears on whether the platform reaches volume customer shipments and first revenue recognition within fiscal 2027; it is a forecast, not confirmed shipment or revenue.\"}]}, {\"id\": \"ev-7\", \"summary\": \"Trefis reports that NVIDIA's operating margin is 65%, its highest in ten years, against a three-year average of 59%.\", \"party\": \"Trefis (analysis of NVIDIA reported figures)\", \"date\": null, \"figures\": [{\"n\": 1, \"measure\": \"operating margin\", \"period\": null, \"value\": \"65%\", \"kind\": \"actual\", \"basis\": \"highest in ten years\", \"party\": \"Trefis (analysis of NVIDIA reported figures)\"}, {\"n\": 2, \"measure\": \"operating margin, three-year average\", \"period\": \"trailing three years\", \"value\": \"59%\", \"kind\": \"actual\", \"basis\": \"three-year average\", \"party\": \"Trefis (analysis of NVIDIA reported figures)\"}], \"links\": \"none\"}]}\n{\"answer\": \"B43\", \"article\": 10, \"s\": [{\"id\": \"e1\", \"summary\": \"NVIDIA management guided revenue for fiscal Q3 2027 to $108 billion, plus or minus 2%.\", \"party\": \"NVIDIA\", \"date\": null, \"figures\": [{\"n\": 1, \"measure\": \"revenue guidance\", \"period\": \"fiscal Q3 2027\", \"value\": \"$108 billion\", \"kind\": \"guidance\", \"basis\": \"plus or minus 2%\", \"party\": \"NVIDIA\"}], \"links\": \"none\"}, {\"id\": \"e2\", \"summary\": \"The consensus forecast for NVIDIA's fiscal Q3 2027 revenue is $111.3 billion, above the top of the company's own guidance range for that quarter.\", \"party\": null, \"date\": null, \"figures\": [{\"n\": 1, \"measure\": \"revenue consensus forecast\", \"period\": \"fiscal Q3 2027\", \"value\": \"$111.3 billion\", \"kind\": \"estimate\", \"basis\": null, \"party\": \"consensus\"}], \"links\": \"none\"}, {\"id\": \"e3\", \"summary\": \"NVIDIA management said fiscal Q2 2027 revenue was $96 billion, more than double the year-earlier figure, with growth accelerating for a fourth consecutive quarter.\", \"party\": \"NVIDIA\", \"date\": null, \"figures\": [{\"n\": 1, \"measure\": \"revenue\", \"period\": \"fiscal Q2 2027\", \"value\": \"$96 billion\", \"kind\": \"actual\", \"basis\": null, \"party\": \"NVIDIA\"}], \"links\": \"none\"}, {\"id\": \"e4\", \"summary\": \"On the fiscal Q2 2027 call, NVIDIA management said inventory rose to $32 billion as the company prepared for the Vera Rubin launch.\", \"party\": \"NVIDIA\", \"date\": null, \"figures\": [{\"n\": 1, \"measure\": \"inventory\", \"period\": \"fiscal Q2 2027\", \"value\": \"$32 billion\", \"kind\": \"actual\", \"basis\": \"company inventory, as stated on the fiscal Q2 2027 call\", \"party\": \"NVIDIA\"}], \"links\": [{\"question\": \"clean-4\", \"new_question\": false, \"reason\": \"Management's statement that inventory rose to $32 billion in preparation for the Vera Rubin launch is a sign the launch is being supplied ahead of shipments; it bears on the question of whether Vera Rubin reaches volume customer shipments and first revenue recognition by end-FY2027, but does not itself confirm shipments or revenue.\"}]}, {\"id\": \"e5\", \"summary\": \"NVIDIA management expects the newly launched Vera Rubin platform to make up about 20% of data center revenue in fiscal Q3 2027.\", \"party\": \"NVIDIA\", \"date\": null, \"figures\": [{\"n\": 1, \"measure\": \"Vera Rubin share of data center revenue\", \"period\": \"fiscal Q3 2027\", \"value\": \"about 20%\", \"kind\": \"guidance\", \"basis\": \"share of data center revenue\", \"party\": \"NVIDIA management\"}], \"links\": [{\"question\": \"clean-4\", \"new_question\": false, \"reason\": \"The company's own forecast that Vera Rubin will supply about 20% of data center revenue in fiscal Q3 2027 implies the platform is expected to generate revenue within FY2027; it bears on whether volume customer shipments and first revenue recognition occur by end-FY2027, though the forecast is not confirmation of shipments.\"}]}, {\"id\": \"e7\", \"summary\": \"NVIDIA guided gross margin for fiscal Q3 2027 to 74%, plus or minus half a point, down from 75% in fiscal Q2 2027, with management pointing to higher component costs, especially memory.\", \"party\": \"NVIDIA\", \"date\": null, \"figures\": [{\"n\": 1, \"measure\": \"gross margin guidance\", \"period\": \"fiscal Q3 2027\", \"value\": \"74%\", \"kind\": \"guidance\", \"basis\": \"plus or minus half a point; company gross margin\", \"party\": \"NVIDIA management\"}, {\"n\": 2, \"measure\": \"gross margin\", \"period\": \"fiscal Q2 2027\", \"value\": \"75%\", \"kind\": \"actual\", \"basis\": \"company gross margin\", \"party\": \"NVIDIA\"}], \"links\": \"none\"}, {\"id\": \"e8\", \"summary\": \"Trefis reports NVIDIA's operating margin at 65%, its highest in ten years, against a three-year average of 59%.\", \"party\": \"trefis.com\", \"date\": null, \"figures\": [{\"n\": 1, \"measure\": \"operating margin\", \"period\": null, \"value\": \"65%\", \"kind\": \"actual\", \"basis\": \"author's calculation from reported results; company operating margin\", \"party\": \"trefis.com\"}, {\"n\": 2, \"measure\": \"operating margin, three-year average\", \"period\": \"three-year average\", \"value\": \"59%\", \"kind\": \"actual\", \"basis\": \"author's calculation; three-year average, company operating margin\", \"party\": \"trefis.com\"}], \"links\": \"none\"}]}\n{\"answer\": \"B46\", \"article\": 5, \"s\": [{\"id\": \"e1\", \"summary\": \"NVIDIA reported $96.2 billion in revenue for its fiscal second quarter, 106% more than in the same period a year earlier, and produced a 75% gross margin, according to the Motley Fool's review of the results.\", \"party\": \"NVIDIA\", \"date\": null, \"figures\": [{\"n\": 1, \"measure\": \"revenue\", \"period\": \"fiscal second quarter\", \"value\": \"$96.2 billion\", \"kind\": \"actual\", \"basis\": \"company-wide revenue, up 106% year over year\", \"party\": \"NVIDIA\"}, {\"n\": 2, \"measure\": \"gross margin\", \"period\": \"fiscal second quarter\", \"value\": \"75%\", \"kind\": \"actual\", \"basis\": \"company-wide gross margin\", \"party\": \"NVIDIA\"}], \"links\": \"none\"}, {\"id\": \"e2\", \"summary\": \"NVIDIA management told investors to expect roughly 70% revenue growth in the next fiscal year, the Motley Fool reported.\", \"party\": \"NVIDIA\", \"date\": null, \"figures\": [{\"n\": 1, \"measure\": \"revenue growth\", \"period\": \"next fiscal year\", \"value\": \"roughly 70%\", \"kind\": \"guidance\", \"basis\": \"management guidance as reported, company-wide\", \"party\": \"NVIDIA\"}], \"links\": \"none\"}, {\"id\": \"e3\", \"summary\": \"NVIDIA guided to $108 billion in revenue for the quarter following the reported fiscal second quarter, representing 17% sequential growth.\", \"party\": \"NVIDIA\", \"date\": null, \"figures\": [{\"n\": 1, \"measure\": \"revenue\", \"period\": \"current quarter (fiscal third quarter)\", \"value\": \"$108 billion\", \"kind\": \"guidance\", \"basis\": \"company guidance, representing 17% sequential growth\", \"party\": \"NVIDIA\"}], \"links\": \"none\"}, {\"id\": \"e4\", \"summary\": \"NVIDIA's total assets jumped more than 50% over the six months to the end of July, with receivables rising from $38.5 billion in January to $63.1 billion at the end of July and days sales outstanding rising from 45 to 60 days in one quarter, according to the Motley Fool's reading of the balance sheet.\", \"party\": \"NVIDIA\", \"date\": null, \"figures\": [{\"n\": 1, \"measure\": \"receivables\", \"period\": \"end of July (fiscal Q2)\", \"value\": \"$63.1 billion\", \"kind\": \"actual\", \"basis\": \"up from $38.5 billion in January\", \"party\": \"NVIDIA\"}, {\"n\": 2, \"measure\": \"days sales outstanding\", \"period\": \"one quarter (fiscal Q2)\", \"value\": \"60 days\", \"kind\": \"actual\", \"basis\": \"up from 45 days in the prior quarter\", \"party\": \"NVIDIA\"}, {\"n\": 3, \"measure\": \"total assets\", \"period\": \"past six months to end of July\", \"value\": \"more than 50%\", \"kind\": \"actual\", \"basis\": \"growth over the six-month period\", \"party\": \"NVIDIA\"}], \"links\": [{\"question\": \"clean-1\", \"new_question\": false, \"reason\": \"Receivables growth and the jump in days sales outstanding from 45 to 60 days bear on how much NVIDIA is financing customers' purchases, the same question as the thesis's guarantees and credit support, though these are unpaid invoices rather than disclosed guarantees.\"}]}, {\"id\": \"e6\", \"summary\": \"NVIDIA agreed to guarantee up to $105 billion of data center lease obligations for OpenAI, the Motley Fool reported as an example of 'circular' financial moves in which NVIDIA helps customers pay for its products.\", \"party\": \"NVIDIA\", \"date\": null, \"figures\": [{\"n\": 1, \"measure\": \"guarantee of data center lease obligations for OpenAI\", \"period\": null, \"value\": \"$105 billion\", \"kind\": \"actual\", \"basis\": \"up to; guarantee of OpenAI's data center lease obligations\", \"party\": \"NVIDIA\"}], \"links\": [{\"question\": \"clean-1\", \"new_question\": false, \"reason\": \"A sized credit-support commitment of exactly the kind the thesis asks NVIDIA to disclose at its next annual report: a guarantee backing another party's lease obligations; the item restates it rather than reporting a new step.\"}]}]}\n{\"answer\": \"B48\", \"article\": 5, \"s\": [{\"id\": \"e1\", \"summary\": \"NVIDIA reported $96.2 billion in revenue for its fiscal second quarter, 106% more than in the same period a year earlier, at a 75% gross margin, according to Fool.com's account of the results.\", \"party\": \"NVIDIA\", \"date\": null, \"figures\": [{\"n\": 1, \"measure\": \"revenue\", \"period\": \"fiscal second quarter\", \"value\": \"$96.2 billion\", \"kind\": \"actual\", \"basis\": null, \"party\": \"NVIDIA\"}, {\"n\": 2, \"measure\": \"revenue growth vs. same period last year\", \"period\": \"fiscal second quarter\", \"value\": \"106%\", \"kind\": \"actual\", \"basis\": null, \"party\": \"NVIDIA\"}, {\"n\": 3, \"measure\": \"gross margin\", \"period\": \"fiscal second quarter\", \"value\": \"75%\", \"kind\": \"actual\", \"basis\": null, \"party\": \"NVIDIA\"}], \"links\": \"none\"}, {\"id\": \"e2\", \"summary\": \"NVIDIA management told investors to expect roughly 70% revenue growth in the next fiscal year, Fool.com reported.\", \"party\": \"NVIDIA\", \"date\": null, \"figures\": [{\"n\": 1, \"measure\": \"revenue growth\", \"period\": \"next fiscal year\", \"value\": \"roughly 70%\", \"kind\": \"guidance\", \"basis\": null, \"party\": \"NVIDIA\"}], \"links\": \"none\"}, {\"id\": \"e3\", \"summary\": \"NVIDIA guided to $108 billion in revenue for the current quarter, representing 17% sequential growth.\", \"party\": \"NVIDIA\", \"date\": null, \"figures\": [{\"n\": 1, \"measure\": \"revenue\", \"period\": \"current quarter\", \"value\": \"$108 billion\", \"kind\": \"guidance\", \"basis\": null, \"party\": \"NVIDIA\"}, {\"n\": 2, \"measure\": \"sequential revenue growth\", \"period\": \"current quarter\", \"value\": \"17%\", \"kind\": \"guidance\", \"basis\": null, \"party\": \"NVIDIA\"}], \"links\": \"none\"}, {\"id\": \"e4\", \"summary\": \"NVIDIA's total assets jumped by more than 50% over the past six months, with receivables rising from $38.5 billion in January to $63.1 billion at the end of July and days sales outstanding rising from 45 to 60 days in one quarter; NVIDIA said it extended payment terms on large orders from certain customers, which the article flags as a sign customers are taking longer to pay.\", \"party\": \"NVIDIA\", \"date\": null, \"figures\": [{\"n\": 1, \"measure\": \"total assets growth\", \"period\": \"past six months\", \"value\": \"more than 50%\", \"kind\": \"actual\", \"basis\": null, \"party\": \"NVIDIA\"}, {\"n\": 2, \"measure\": \"accounts receivable\", \"period\": \"January\", \"value\": \"$38.5 billion\", \"kind\": \"actual\", \"basis\": null, \"party\": \"NVIDIA\"}, {\"n\": 3, \"measure\": \"accounts receivable\", \"period\": \"end of July\", \"value\": \"$63.1 billion\", \"kind\": \"actual\", \"basis\": null, \"party\": \"NVIDIA\"}, {\"n\": 4, \"measure\": \"days sales outstanding (DSO)\", \"period\": \"one quarter\", \"value\": \"45 to 60 days\", \"kind\": \"actual\", \"basis\": null, \"party\": \"NVIDIA\"}], \"links\": [{\"question\": \"clean-1\", \"new_question\": false, \"reason\": \"NVIDIA's extension of payment terms on large customer orders is a form of trade credit/customer financing, relevant to the thesis's question of how much credit support NVIDIA extends to the AI labs and cloud partners it sells to; the item discloses no amount of terms extended and this is trade credit, not a guarantee or revenue backstop.\"}]}, {\"id\": \"e5\", \"summary\": \"NVIDIA agreed to guarantee up to $105 billion of data center lease obligations for OpenAI, cited by Fool.com as part of a string of 'circular' financial moves in which NVIDIA helps customers pay for its products; the item gives no date for the agreement.\", \"party\": \"NVIDIA\", \"date\": null, \"figures\": [{\"n\": 1, \"measure\": \"guarantee of OpenAI data center lease obligations\", \"period\": null, \"value\": \"up to $105 billion\", \"kind\": \"actual\", \"basis\": \"up to\", \"party\": \"NVIDIA\"}], \"links\": [{\"question\": \"clean-1\", \"new_question\": false, \"reason\": \"Sizes NVIDIA's guarantee of OpenAI's data center lease obligations at up to $105 billion — exactly the kind of credit-support commitment the thesis asks NVIDIA to disclose at its next annual report; the item does not say whether or how the guarantee has been reported in filings.\"}]}, {\"id\": \"e6\", \"summary\": \"NVIDIA has formed partnerships to raise over $500 billion of outside capital for AI infrastructure, according to Fool.com, which cites this alongside the OpenAI lease guarantee as evidence NVIDIA is helping customers pay for its products; the item gives no date or partner names.\", \"party\": \"NVIDIA\", \"date\": null, \"figures\": [{\"n\": 1, \"measure\": \"outside capital raised for AI infrastructure through partnerships\", \"period\": null, \"value\": \"over $500 billion\", \"kind\": \"actual\", \"basis\": null, \"party\": \"NVIDIA\"}], \"links\": [{\"question\": \"clean-1\", \"new_question\": false, \"reason\": \"Gives the scale (over $500 billion) of outside capital NVIDIA's partnerships raise for AI infrastructure; this is third-party capital rather than NVIDIA's own guarantee, but it bears on the thesis's comparison of guarantees/credit support versus equity and other support NVIDIA extends to AI labs and cloud partners.\"}]}]}\n{\"answer\": \"B56\", \"article\": 9, \"s\": [{\"id\": \"ev-1\", \"summary\": \"Zacks Consensus Estimates project Nvidia's upcoming-quarter EPS at $2.47, a 90.00% increase versus the same quarter a year earlier, and revenue at $109.02 billion, a 91.24% increase versus the year-ago quarter, according to the latest consensus cited by Zacks.\", \"party\": \"Zacks Investment Research (Zacks Consensus Estimates)\", \"date\": \"2026-09-28\", \"figures\": [{\"n\": 1, \"measure\": \"EPS\", \"period\": \"upcoming quarter (quarter not named)\", \"value\": \"$2.47\", \"kind\": \"estimate\", \"basis\": \"Zacks Consensus Estimate; vs. same quarter of previous year (+90.00%)\", \"party\": \"Zacks Investment Research (Zacks Consensus Estimates)\"}, {\"n\": 2, \"measure\": \"Revenue\", \"period\": \"upcoming quarter (quarter not named)\", \"value\": \"$109.02 billion\", \"kind\": \"estimate\", \"basis\": \"Zacks Consensus Estimate; vs. year-ago quarter (+91.24%)\", \"party\": \"Zacks Investment Research (Zacks Consensus Estimates)\"}], \"links\": \"none\"}, {\"id\": \"ev-2\", \"summary\": \"Zacks Consensus Estimates project Nvidia's full-year earnings at $9.25 per share and revenue at $406.05 billion, which would represent increases of 93.92% and 88.04%, respectively, from the prior year.\", \"party\": \"Zacks Investment Research (Zacks Consensus Estimates)\", \"date\": \"2026-09-28\", \"figures\": [{\"n\": 1, \"measure\": \"Earnings per share\", \"period\": \"full year (fiscal year not specified)\", \"value\": \"$9.25 per share\", \"kind\": \"estimate\", \"basis\": \"Zacks Consensus Estimate; +93.92% vs. prior year\", \"party\": \"Zacks Investment Research (Zacks Consensus Estimates)\"}, {\"n\": 2, \"measure\": \"Revenue\", \"period\": \"full year (fiscal year not specified)\", \"value\": \"$406.05 billion\", \"kind\": \"estimate\", \"basis\": \"Zacks Consensus Estimate; +88.04% vs. prior year\", \"party\": \"Zacks Investment Research (Zacks Consensus Estimates)\"}], \"links\": \"none\"}, {\"id\": \"ev-3\", \"summary\": \"The Zacks Consensus EPS estimate for Nvidia has been revised 4.73% upward over the past month, per Zacks.\", \"party\": \"Zacks Investment Research (Zacks Consensus Estimates)\", \"date\": \"2026-09-28\", \"figures\": [{\"n\": 1, \"measure\": \"Zacks Consensus EPS estimate revision\", \"period\": \"past month\", \"value\": \"4.73%\", \"kind\": \"estimate\", \"basis\": \"Upward revision of consensus EPS over the past month\", \"party\": \"Zacks Investment Research (Zacks Consensus Estimates)\"}], \"links\": \"none\"}]}\n{\"answer\": \"B58\", \"article\": 9, \"s\": [{\"id\": \"e1\", \"summary\": \"The Zacks Consensus Estimates cited in the article project Nvidia's upcoming-quarter EPS at $2.47, a 90.00% increase versus the same quarter a year earlier, and upcoming-quarter revenue at $109.02 billion, a 91.24% increase versus the year-ago quarter.\", \"party\": \"Zacks Consensus Estimates (Zacks Investment Research)\", \"date\": null, \"figures\": [{\"n\": 1, \"measure\": \"EPS (Zacks Consensus Estimate)\", \"period\": \"upcoming quarter\", \"value\": \"$2.47\", \"kind\": \"estimate\", \"basis\": \"consensus estimate; +90.00% versus the same quarter of the previous year\", \"party\": \"Zacks Consensus Estimates (Zacks Investment Research)\"}, {\"n\": 2, \"measure\": \"revenue (Zacks Consensus Estimate)\", \"period\": \"upcoming quarter\", \"value\": \"$109.02 billion\", \"kind\": \"estimate\", \"basis\": \"consensus estimate; +91.24% versus the year-ago quarter\", \"party\": \"Zacks Consensus Estimates (Zacks Investment Research)\"}], \"links\": \"none\"}, {\"id\": \"e2\", \"summary\": \"The Zacks Consensus Estimates cited in the article project Nvidia's full-year earnings at $9.25 per share, +93.92% versus the prior year, and full-year revenue at $406.05 billion, +88.04% versus the prior year.\", \"party\": \"Zacks Consensus Estimates (Zacks Investment Research)\", \"date\": null, \"figures\": [{\"n\": 1, \"measure\": \"earnings per share (Zacks Consensus Estimate)\", \"period\": \"full year\", \"value\": \"$9.25 per share\", \"kind\": \"estimate\", \"basis\": \"consensus estimate; +93.92% versus the prior year\", \"party\": \"Zacks Consensus Estimates (Zacks Investment Research)\"}, {\"n\": 2, \"measure\": \"revenue (Zacks Consensus Estimate)\", \"period\": \"full year\", \"value\": \"$406.05 billion\", \"kind\": \"estimate\", \"basis\": \"consensus estimate; +88.04% versus the prior year\", \"party\": \"Zacks Consensus Estimates (Zacks Investment Research)\"}], \"links\": \"none\"}, {\"id\": \"e3\", \"summary\": \"Zacks reports that its Consensus EPS estimate for Nvidia has been revised 4.73% upward over the past month, a direction it reads as analysts turning more positive on the near-term business, and that Nvidia currently carries a Zacks Rank of #1 (Strong Buy).\", \"party\": \"Zacks Investment Research\", \"date\": \"past month (relative date, as stated)\", \"figures\": [{\"n\": 1, \"measure\": \"Zacks Consensus EPS estimate revision\", \"period\": \"past month\", \"value\": \"4.73%\", \"kind\": \"estimate\", \"basis\": \"upward shift in the consensus EPS estimate over the past month\", \"party\": \"Zacks Investment Research\"}], \"links\": \"none\"}]}\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
